WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:04.080
- Alright, so first of
all, welcome to all of you.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:05.520
I'm sorry that we're a little delayed,

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:09.090
but we're really fortunate
that the director was able to,

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:09.930
with the commissioner,

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.060
participate in an event with our state,

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:15.750
with our senator, our mayor,
our governor at Fairhaven

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:17.430
with the groundbreaking
of the new building

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:19.500
at the Fairhaven Community Health Center.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.229
I'm Howie Foreman from the
Department of Radiology.

00:00:22.229 --> 00:00:24.690
And on behalf of the
Department of Radiology

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:25.830
and the School of Public Health,

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:27.180
I really wanna welcome all of you

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:28.928
and appreciate your patience.

00:00:28.928 --> 00:00:30.968
We have a great event ahead of you.

00:00:30.968 --> 00:00:33.008
This is a collaboration between

00:00:33.008 --> 00:00:35.061
the Department of Radiology

00:00:35.061 --> 00:00:37.680
and the Boroff-Foreman Lecture,

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.450
as well as the Yale
School of Public Health,

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:40.910
the Dean's Lecture Series.

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:42.270
This could not have happened

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:44.340
without the sincere and complete support

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:46.028
of both of those entities.

00:00:46.028 --> 00:00:48.630
And at this time there's a
million people I'd like to thank,

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:51.330
but for the most part I want
to thank our speakers today

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:53.253
and I'll do so now.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:57.120
I'm gonna hand it over in a
minute to our commissioner

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:58.830
to introduce our speakers.

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:01.620
I'm also grateful to
our dean, Megan Ranney.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.030
I'm grateful to Mandy Cohen,

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:06.090
who is our Yale alum who
committed to this event

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:06.930
seven months ago.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:07.890
And we thought it would be

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:09.631
a few little simple small events

00:01:09.631 --> 00:01:11.550
and then things intervened.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:14.640
I'm also convinced that
she and our other guests

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.290
have helped us avoid
a government shutdown,

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:18.473
because otherwise we
couldn't have done this.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:22.890
Also thankful the Solomon
Center at the Yale Law School

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:25.909
under Abby Gluck and
the Yale Medical Council

00:01:25.909 --> 00:01:28.350
with leadership from Wilton Sun

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:30.360
have been really instrumental
in making us be able

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.040
to pull this event off.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:33.854
Cannot thank all the CDC staff,

00:01:33.854 --> 00:01:36.606
but I do wanna particularly
thank Ursula Shell

00:01:36.606 --> 00:01:40.140
for her work in
collaborating on this event.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:41.880
So Dr. Manisha Juthani,

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.530
who will soon take the microphone

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.330
and introduce our speakers,

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:47.760
is a Yale faculty member on leave.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.751
Having taken on the
enormous responsibility

00:01:49.751 --> 00:01:52.290
of Commissioner of the Connecticut

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:53.670
Department of Public Health,

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:56.610
a position that she has held
over these last two years,

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:59.550
including helping us steer
through the end of the pandemic.

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:03.240
Dr. Juthani is a world renowned
infectious disease clinician

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.535
and scholar and has
been a forceful advocate

00:02:05.535 --> 00:02:08.687
for the ideal of healthcare
as a human right.

00:02:08.687 --> 00:02:11.610
Her work is infused with a commitment

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:13.500
to health equity and she helps us tackle

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:16.650
some of the greatest public
health challenges of our lives.

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:18.420
She's a graduate of Penn, Cornell

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:19.680
and trained partly at Yale

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.450
before joining our faculty.

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.040
I will turn it over to her

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:25.110
and I wanna just thank
everybody in advance.

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:26.860
Thank you very much for doing this.

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:34.343
- Thank you very much

00:02:34.343 --> 00:02:36.570
for that introduction, Dr. Foreman.

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:39.030
And it's a great honor to be back at Yale

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:41.160
as always and to be here

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.311
with these esteemed speakers here today.

00:02:43.311 --> 00:02:45.711
So I want to introduce our two speakers.

00:02:45.711 --> 00:02:49.020
Our first is Dr. Mandy Cohen,

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:50.160
who is the director of

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.530
the Centers for Disease
Control and Prevention

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:53.400
and the administrator of

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:56.910
the Agency for Toxic Substances
and Disease Registry.

00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:59.040
She's one of the nation's
top health leaders

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.791
with experience leading large
and complex organizations

00:03:01.791 --> 00:03:03.720
and a proven track record

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:06.660
protecting Americans' health and safety.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:09.151
Dr. Cohen received her
bachelor's degree from Cornell,

00:03:09.151 --> 00:03:11.490
her doctor of medicine from right here,

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:12.720
Yale School of Medicine.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.133
Great to welcome her back.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:19.560
I'm sure that's when
she'd befriended Howie.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:22.620
And you know, her
master's in Public Health

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:24.630
from the Harvard School of Public Health.

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:26.160
She trained in internal medicine

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:28.500
at Massachusetts General Hospital.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.930
Dr. Cohen led the North
Carolina Department

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:32.610
of Health and Human Services

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:35.370
where she was lauded for
her outstanding leadership

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:36.870
during the covid crisis,

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:39.450
focusing on equity, data accountability

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:41.520
and transparent communication.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:44.527
She also transforms the North
Carolina Medicaid program

00:03:44.527 --> 00:03:46.710
through the state's Medicaid expansion

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:49.950
and her focus on whole person health

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:51.810
with the launch of the country's first

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:56.190
statewide coordination
platform, NCCARE360.

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:58.290
Prior to joining CDC, Dr. Cohen served

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:01.830
as the Executive Vice
President at Aledade,

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:04.530
the CEO of Aledade Care Solutions.

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:06.690
And Dr. Cohen was involved in many aspects

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:09.330
of the Affordable Care
Act policy development

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:11.940
and implementation during her time serving

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:14.670
as Chief Operating
Officer and Chief of Staff

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:17.340
of the Centers for Medicare
and Medicaid Services,

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:19.800
as well as Acting Director of the Center

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:22.740
for Consumer Information
and Insurance Oversight.

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:24.600
It's with this outstanding background

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:25.560
that we are so fortunate

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.573
to have her now leading the CDC.

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:31.440
And now to Dr. Megan Ranney,

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.230
who is the dean of the Yale
School of Public Health

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:37.470
and the CEA Winslow
Professor of Public Health.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.660
She's an emergency physician, researcher

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.010
and national advocate

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:43.860
for innovative approaches
to public health.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:46.410
Dr. Ranney earned her
bachelor's degree from Harvard,

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:48.150
her medical doctorate from Columbia

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:51.510
and her master's degree in
public health from Brown.

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:53.760
She completed her residency
in emergency medicine

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:55.770
and fellowship in injury
prevention research

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:57.150
at Brown University.

00:04:57.150 --> 00:04:59.850
And she was previously
a Peace Corps volunteer.

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:01.980
Prior to arriving at Yale, Dr. Ranney

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:03.390
served as deputy dean

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:05.670
at the Brown University
School of Public Health,

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:08.730
the Warren Alpert Endowed
Professor of Emergency Medicine

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:11.280
at Alpert Medical School
of Brown University

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:12.630
and was the founding director

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:15.660
of the Brown Lifespan
Center for Digital Health.

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:18.232
Dr. Ranney's research
focuses on developing testing

00:05:18.232 --> 00:05:20.850
and disseminating digital
health interventions

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:24.510
to prevent violence and related
behavioral health problems,

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:28.560
as well as on the COVID
related risk reduction.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:30.930
She's held multiple
national leadership roles,

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:34.350
including co-founding and
senior strategic advisor

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:35.520
for the American Foundation

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:38.100
for Firearm injury reduction in Medicine

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:39.480
at the Aspen Institute.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:42.652
A nonprofit committed to ending
the gun violence epidemic

00:05:42.652 --> 00:05:45.450
through a non-partisan
public health approach.

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:48.570
And being co-founder of Get Us PPE,

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:53.010
a startup nonprofit that
donated and delivered PPE

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:54.870
to those who needed it most.

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:58.650
She's an elected member of the
National Academy of Medicine.

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:00.785
It's really an honor to
have them both here today.

00:06:00.785 --> 00:06:03.600
I'd like to say as all of us,

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:06.360
all three of us being mother of two women,

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:08.792
as women being mothers
of two children each.

00:06:08.792 --> 00:06:10.320
(Manisha laughing)

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:12.780
Got tongue tied on my words.

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:14.370
But you know, it's really remarkable

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:15.930
to see these women in leadership

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:18.030
with this outstanding background

00:06:18.030 --> 00:06:21.320
and we are so excited to hear
their conversation today.

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:23.493
- Thank you, thank you Dr. Juthani.

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:32.370
So thank you for joining us today.

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:34.350
I do wanna apologize again for the wait.

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:35.490
Thank you for your patience.

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:37.413
Great things come to those who wait.

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:39.960
And thank you Dr. Cohen

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:42.720
for joining us all the way from Atlanta.

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:45.390
In my conversations with
faculty, staff, and students,

00:06:45.390 --> 00:06:47.400
I know how much so many of you

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:49.260
have been looking forward to this day.

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:50.310
I know there are many others

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:52.110
who are unable to attend in person,

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:54.990
but are looking forward to
watching the recording on Zoom.

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:57.210
And we're grateful for your
coming back to New Haven,

00:06:57.210 --> 00:06:59.250
a place that you once called home.

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:00.120
So, as you all know,

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:02.730
I launched the Leaders
in Public Health series

00:07:02.730 --> 00:07:04.350
as one of the first event series

00:07:04.350 --> 00:07:07.650
of my deanship here at Yale
School of Public Health.

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:09.376
I, like so many of you in the audience,

00:07:09.376 --> 00:07:11.425
believe deeply in the power

00:07:11.425 --> 00:07:14.670
of extraordinary public health leaders.

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:16.710
And I'm committed to
bringing these leaders,

00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:19.050
thinkers, and doers here to Yale

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:21.660
to share with us a little
bit about their career path,

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:24.570
lessons learned and providing inspiration

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:26.730
for those, particularly students,

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:30.300
who are going to go out and
be change makers themselves.

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:32.100
As we pioneer together,

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:35.220
a new era of the Yale School
of Public Health's independence

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:37.500
will continue to have
speakers and collaborators

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:41.580
that are doing work centered
around inclusion and community,

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:44.010
innovation and entrepreneurship,

00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:47.370
communication and of course
data-driven leadership.

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:48.570
Today's event with Mandy

00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:51.390
is only the second of what
I hope will be a long series

00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:53.520
of bringing our community together

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:55.860
across the school of public
health, the school of Medicine,

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:57.063
and the rest of campus.

00:07:58.170 --> 00:08:00.600
Before we get started, I
do have a little surprise.

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:02.100
Those who came to my last deans lecture

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:03.180
will have a heads up.

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:05.580
I want you to look under your seat.

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:07.830
Eight lucky people have a small memento

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:09.033
to remember this event.

00:08:10.290 --> 00:08:12.930
There are some empty
seats, so take a look.

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:13.883
- That is so Oprah of you, I love it.

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:17.073
- Who got them?

00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:20.070
- Got them.

00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:21.150
Show us your...

00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:24.153
- Show us show us the little swag.

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:26.493
- There you go.

00:08:26.493 --> 00:08:30.450
- Got some mugs in the back down here.

00:08:30.450 --> 00:08:31.770
Excellent.

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:33.810
So congratulations to you.

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:36.063
A memory of Dr. Cohen's visit.

00:08:38.910 --> 00:08:40.560
So without further ado,

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:42.420
I'm gonna move us into the discussion.

00:08:42.420 --> 00:08:44.160
Dr. Cohen, it is such a privilege

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:46.020
and an honor to have you here.

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:47.070
We're honored you,

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:48.690
we got to share the stage sometimes

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:50.790
over the course of the covid 19 pandemic.

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:54.270
I've long been an admirer
and an acquaintance

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:55.830
and I'm just really
thrilled to have you here

00:08:55.830 --> 00:08:56.850
joining us today.

00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:57.683
- Thank you.

00:08:58.530 --> 00:08:59.640
- So I'm gonna start at the beginning,

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:02.580
not going back to the beginning
of your time here at Yale,

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:04.290
but rather the beginning of your time

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:08.190
as North Carolina Secretary of DHHS.

00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:11.580
I call that out because as Dr.
Juthani can no doubt attest,

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:12.630
there are some unique things

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:14.310
that you learn in public health.

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:16.608
As the head of a state health agency.

00:09:16.608 --> 00:09:18.240
Wondered if you'd mind sharing

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:21.693
with our esteemed group of
students, faculty and staff,

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:25.080
how your understanding of public health

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:27.270
and the role of that
intersection of government,

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:28.980
private sector and nonprofits

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:31.260
changed over your tenure as secretary.

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:32.093
- Sure.

00:09:32.093 --> 00:09:33.720
Well, first before I,

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:34.553
oh, can you all hear me?

00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:37.512
Before I dive in,

00:09:37.512 --> 00:09:38.550
I do wanna say thanks for having me back.

00:09:38.550 --> 00:09:40.290
It's wonderful to be back in New Haven

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:41.520
and it's been a wonderful day

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:43.633
of being at the Fairhaven Clinic

00:09:43.633 --> 00:09:46.320
and seeing them do a groundbreaking,

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:47.959
if they're expanding what they're doing.

00:09:47.959 --> 00:09:52.959
And I think that my education here at Yale

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:56.130
was really pivotal for me.

00:09:56.130 --> 00:10:00.120
It allowed me as a student to think big.

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:03.162
And it was the first
time where folks said,

00:10:03.162 --> 00:10:07.440
it's okay to think about
how do we want the world

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:08.820
to be different and how are you

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:11.496
going to fit into that space.

00:10:11.496 --> 00:10:13.260
As a first year medical student,

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:16.320
Yale supported us to go
over to South Africa,

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:17.580
a bunch of students

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:20.550
and actually get hands-on
experience in global health

00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:23.880
working on the HIV crisis at the time.

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:26.730
And it was just the start of a journey

00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:31.140
thinking about where do
I fit in to all of that.

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:34.856
And I've been incredibly
lucky to have taken,

00:10:34.856 --> 00:10:37.136
I'm grateful to have taken some risks

00:10:37.136 --> 00:10:39.690
actually early in my career to say,

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:44.400
I want to think about leading
in the public sector space.

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:47.910
And then sometimes you also get lucky.

00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:49.650
I was also in the right
place at the right time

00:10:49.650 --> 00:10:52.110
with the Affordable
Care Act getting passed.

00:10:52.110 --> 00:10:55.080
Frankly, I was in the right
place when CMS entered

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:57.600
a crisis when healthcare.gov
didn't turn on.

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:59.340
You might not, you all might be too young

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:02.580
to remember in 2013, that crisis.

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:04.230
But all of these were
incredibly formative.

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:08.390
So that when I came to
North Carolina in 2017

00:11:08.390 --> 00:11:10.800
and I lived there for five years,

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:12.840
I was brand new to North Carolina.

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:15.120
I'm from New York originally,

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:20.120
and it was, North Carolina
is in a remarkable place.

00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:22.650
And now my family continues to live there

00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:24.573
and I'm from North Carolina now.

00:11:25.890 --> 00:11:29.310
After you go through leading
through a crisis like covid,

00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:31.290
you bond with the state.

00:11:31.290 --> 00:11:35.070
But it isn't, I loved
being there, but it was,

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:38.698
it was a different role
going to lead at the state,

00:11:38.698 --> 00:11:41.430
at the state level than
the work I had done

00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:42.330
at the federal government.

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:44.906
At the state level, you both get to build

00:11:44.906 --> 00:11:48.218
much more authentic relationships.

00:11:48.218 --> 00:11:51.480
You see the problems up close and personal

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:52.710
in a different way.

00:11:52.710 --> 00:11:56.762
And North Carolina's a
challenging place to do work.

00:11:56.762 --> 00:11:58.950
I worked for a Democratic governor.

00:11:58.950 --> 00:12:03.660
We had a Republican super
majority in our legislature,

00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:05.880
so a divided government.

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:10.880
We were very, have a very
stark divide, urban/rural

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:13.658
in North Carolina.

00:12:13.658 --> 00:12:17.160
And so it was a really
interesting place to work.

00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:18.390
But also I went there

00:12:18.390 --> 00:12:20.880
to be Secretary of Health
and Human Services.

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:23.220
So I thought that was
an exciting challenge

00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:26.670
because it wasn't just the
director of public health

00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:29.160
or the head of Medicaid or
the head of mental health.

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:31.253
I got to think about knitting together

00:12:31.253 --> 00:12:35.040
all of those health and human services

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:36.570
in service of health.

00:12:36.570 --> 00:12:38.490
And we really kept that as our north star.

00:12:38.490 --> 00:12:40.650
Really, how can we deliver health

00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:42.482
for the people of North Carolina?

00:12:42.482 --> 00:12:44.670
So as I think about public health,

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:46.170
I think that's what we're
trying to do, right?

00:12:46.170 --> 00:12:47.520
Is think about populations

00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:49.350
and how do we make them healthier.

00:12:49.350 --> 00:12:51.242
And what that taught me

00:12:51.242 --> 00:12:55.110
was you cannot do that
alone from public health.

00:12:55.110 --> 00:12:57.146
I think if I had tried to just do that

00:12:57.146 --> 00:12:59.520
from the perch of public health

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:01.320
without having the benefit

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:04.350
of thinking about the levers
of payment through Medicaid

00:13:04.350 --> 00:13:06.270
or the service delivery and mental health,

00:13:06.270 --> 00:13:09.870
or the social services that we had there.

00:13:09.870 --> 00:13:13.680
We couldn't have put together
that comprehensive view

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:15.390
of trying to drive towards health.

00:13:15.390 --> 00:13:17.850
So I felt pretty lucky
that before the pandemic,

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:19.835
I had three years of thinking about

00:13:19.835 --> 00:13:21.540
how do we buy health.

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:24.390
I had a $20 billion a year budget,

00:13:24.390 --> 00:13:26.943
which I will say is a
bigger than the CDC budget.

00:13:28.086 --> 00:13:30.120
Yes, we get to, not kidding.

00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:31.946
- Sadly. Yeah.
- We'll get to that.

00:13:31.946 --> 00:13:33.870
So a pretty large budget

00:13:33.870 --> 00:13:36.330
when you think about all of
those comprehensive services

00:13:36.330 --> 00:13:38.040
and when you ask that question

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:41.010
of each and every one of those levers

00:13:41.010 --> 00:13:43.290
from social services to mental health,

00:13:43.290 --> 00:13:45.150
to public health to Medicaid,

00:13:45.150 --> 00:13:48.090
to say how do we deliver and
buy health for this state?

00:13:48.090 --> 00:13:49.410
It was pretty powerful.

00:13:49.410 --> 00:13:52.522
And so I love being able to
bring together those levers.

00:13:52.522 --> 00:13:55.378
So when I think about what did
I learn about public health,

00:13:55.378 --> 00:13:57.003
it's a team sport.

00:13:57.870 --> 00:14:00.986
And now being able to then take that,

00:14:00.986 --> 00:14:03.990
what we were doing in terms
of building towards health

00:14:03.990 --> 00:14:06.586
and then take that into
the crisis of the pandemic

00:14:06.586 --> 00:14:08.370
was really important

00:14:08.370 --> 00:14:10.710
because that means when
we went into the pandemic,

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:13.680
we were thinking about whole person health

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:16.290
in our response from day one.

00:14:16.290 --> 00:14:18.698
And I was so lucky to work for,

00:14:18.698 --> 00:14:22.530
I think the best governor
in the country, Roy Cooper.

00:14:22.530 --> 00:14:25.140
He is just, I could not have asked

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:30.026
for a better boss and partner
in that response work.

00:14:30.026 --> 00:14:33.570
And he called out right from the beginning

00:14:33.570 --> 00:14:35.820
that trust was gonna be
the most important thing

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:38.640
that we could focus on
as we go into the crisis.

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:40.920
How do we build trust and
how do we maintain trust

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:42.510
with the people of North Carolina?

00:14:42.510 --> 00:14:44.378
And that's really has shaped me

00:14:44.378 --> 00:14:47.220
as I think about public health, right?

00:14:47.220 --> 00:14:49.410
If we want the world to be different,

00:14:49.410 --> 00:14:51.510
if we want the world to be healthier,

00:14:51.510 --> 00:14:56.280
it always, for me comes back
time and time again to trust.

00:14:56.280 --> 00:14:58.380
And trust, yes is a feeling,

00:14:58.380 --> 00:14:59.880
but I'm sure we'll get into it,

00:14:59.880 --> 00:15:02.642
but trust is a tactical plan

00:15:02.642 --> 00:15:06.002
that you have to execute
on every single day.

00:15:06.002 --> 00:15:08.610
And that's a lot of what I learned

00:15:08.610 --> 00:15:10.740
about leading through
a public health crisis

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:12.090
at the state level.

00:15:12.090 --> 00:15:16.020
And certainly a lot of what
I bring to the CDC work

00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:17.798
as I'm three months in now.

00:15:17.798 --> 00:15:21.000
- I love that the idea of
trust being a tactical plan.

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:23.400
You and I were just talking
about kind of the challenge

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:25.301
of so much of public
health is thinking about

00:15:25.301 --> 00:15:27.990
who are the community partners?

00:15:27.990 --> 00:15:29.940
How do we define the problems?

00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:31.980
How do we describe accurately

00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:35.070
the breadth, depth and hotspots?

00:15:35.070 --> 00:15:38.400
But then you have to have a
plan for what to do afterwards.

00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:39.930
And that's the other side of public health

00:15:39.930 --> 00:15:41.910
that we so often don't talk about.

00:15:41.910 --> 00:15:43.680
But that I think is one
of the special sauces

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:46.802
of certainly the YSM YSPH collaboration,

00:15:46.802 --> 00:15:48.540
the collaboration of YSPH

00:15:48.540 --> 00:15:50.313
with our school of management here.

00:15:51.355 --> 00:15:52.200
And, and thanks to Howie as well,

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:53.850
the combination that collaboration

00:15:53.850 --> 00:15:56.760
between the med school and
the school of management.

00:15:56.760 --> 00:15:59.098
In terms of, I'm gonna
go on that trust theme.

00:15:59.098 --> 00:16:01.710
We all cheered you on when you got

00:16:01.710 --> 00:16:03.450
the directorship of the CDC.

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:05.190
It's just such an incredible honor

00:16:05.190 --> 00:16:07.830
and I can't imagine anyone
better in this country

00:16:07.830 --> 00:16:09.810
to lead the organization.

00:16:09.810 --> 00:16:11.220
I know that recreating trust

00:16:11.220 --> 00:16:13.740
has been a linchpin of what
you've been talking about.

00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:16.920
You wrote a recent editorial
in "The New York Times"

00:16:16.920 --> 00:16:18.870
trying to recreate trust in vaccines

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:20.520
and talking about how
you were getting yourself

00:16:20.520 --> 00:16:22.727
as well as your children vaccinated.

00:16:22.727 --> 00:16:24.990
I would love for you to share

00:16:24.990 --> 00:16:27.000
a little bit about how
you think about that work

00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:29.220
within the CDC, as well as some of your

00:16:29.220 --> 00:16:33.300
other hopes and dreams for
your directorship at the CDC.

00:16:33.300 --> 00:16:34.350
- Sure.

00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:37.590
I feel like I got a masterclass
in thinking about trust,

00:16:37.590 --> 00:16:39.600
like I said, working for Governor Cooper

00:16:39.600 --> 00:16:41.340
and leading through the covid crisis.

00:16:41.340 --> 00:16:43.530
And what I took away from that

00:16:43.530 --> 00:16:45.120
around the tactical plan for trust

00:16:45.120 --> 00:16:49.230
are really three buckets of work.

00:16:49.230 --> 00:16:50.834
The first is transparency,

00:16:50.834 --> 00:16:54.600
the second is simplicity in operations,

00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:57.480
and I'll get to that, or great execution.

00:16:57.480 --> 00:16:59.682
And the third is relationships.

00:16:59.682 --> 00:17:01.920
So on the first is transparency.

00:17:01.920 --> 00:17:05.610
I think the critical
linchpin of public health

00:17:05.610 --> 00:17:10.140
is to bring visibility, data and evidence

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:12.870
to trying to solve how we protect health.

00:17:12.870 --> 00:17:15.120
Whether it's identifying and responding

00:17:15.120 --> 00:17:17.100
to a respiratory virus

00:17:17.100 --> 00:17:21.480
or our first cases of
domestically acquired malaria,

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:25.320
which we had this year, first in 20 years.

00:17:25.320 --> 00:17:28.470
Or it's understanding overdoses

00:17:28.470 --> 00:17:32.910
from different types of
fentanyl or xylazine, right?

00:17:32.910 --> 00:17:36.240
Having that data, that transparency

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:38.340
is critical to building trust.

00:17:38.340 --> 00:17:41.760
So transparency for us was
absolutely fundamental.

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:46.260
And what's hard about transparency

00:17:46.260 --> 00:17:50.010
is science is constantly
evolving as we know.

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:51.180
We are learning more things.

00:17:51.180 --> 00:17:53.190
So it's really critical to be able to say,

00:17:53.190 --> 00:17:54.630
this is what I know,

00:17:54.630 --> 00:17:56.340
this is what I don't know,

00:17:56.340 --> 00:17:58.770
and importantly, this is
what I'm working on to know.

00:17:58.770 --> 00:18:00.086
And I'll come back to
you and you can trust

00:18:00.086 --> 00:18:04.050
that this public health
is not a black box.

00:18:04.050 --> 00:18:05.820
And I can't tell you still,

00:18:05.820 --> 00:18:08.610
I was just with round table
of reporters yesterday

00:18:08.610 --> 00:18:11.550
who said that public health
is a black box to us.

00:18:11.550 --> 00:18:13.170
Help us understand it better.

00:18:13.170 --> 00:18:14.760
We want to be your champions,

00:18:14.760 --> 00:18:15.913
but we don't understand it, let us in.

00:18:15.913 --> 00:18:19.440
All right, so transparency,
I think builds trust.

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:22.200
And in North Carolina, leading
through the covid crisis,

00:18:22.200 --> 00:18:24.510
we did a press conference every day

00:18:24.510 --> 00:18:26.970
at two o'clock for six months,

00:18:26.970 --> 00:18:30.030
and then we did it every other
day for another six months.

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:31.860
And then we did it three times a week

00:18:31.860 --> 00:18:33.120
or two times a week for that, right?

00:18:33.120 --> 00:18:35.720
We did 170 press conferences.

00:18:35.720 --> 00:18:38.640
I never thought I'd be that
person pointing to charts

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:41.280
and graphs on television every day.

00:18:41.280 --> 00:18:42.600
But that transparency,

00:18:42.600 --> 00:18:45.540
that that being there
willing to take questions

00:18:45.540 --> 00:18:46.740
is really, really important.

00:18:46.740 --> 00:18:48.570
And public health needs to both,

00:18:48.570 --> 00:18:52.020
I think have simple and clear messages,

00:18:52.020 --> 00:18:54.485
transparency in its data
as we're doing work.

00:18:54.485 --> 00:18:56.220
Okay, that's one.

00:18:56.220 --> 00:18:59.820
Then second, and this is, I
really feel strongly about.

00:18:59.820 --> 00:19:03.600
In North Carolina, I
was not only the person

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:06.150
who was helping the public understand

00:19:06.150 --> 00:19:08.310
the science and the data

00:19:08.310 --> 00:19:10.320
for why we were making certain decisions,

00:19:10.320 --> 00:19:13.230
I was also responsible for the execution

00:19:13.230 --> 00:19:16.050
or the operations of the response.

00:19:16.050 --> 00:19:18.360
Were there tests in my community?

00:19:18.360 --> 00:19:20.400
Was there PPE for folks?

00:19:20.400 --> 00:19:24.180
Was there vaccine when you
said it was going to be there?

00:19:24.180 --> 00:19:26.250
The execution, because we can talk

00:19:26.250 --> 00:19:27.900
and help people understand it,

00:19:27.900 --> 00:19:30.630
but if they go and try to get a vaccine

00:19:30.630 --> 00:19:32.067
and they've taken an hour off of work

00:19:32.067 --> 00:19:34.920
and their appointment got canceled,

00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:37.050
we've missed an opportunity

00:19:37.050 --> 00:19:39.690
and we can study vaccines
and their effectiveness,

00:19:39.690 --> 00:19:43.800
but if they sit on the
shelf, they're not effective.

00:19:43.800 --> 00:19:46.650
And message is part of that communications

00:19:46.650 --> 00:19:48.150
that's simple and trusted,

00:19:48.150 --> 00:19:50.550
but it's also the operations
and the execution.

00:19:50.550 --> 00:19:51.990
And for me, I'm an operator.

00:19:51.990 --> 00:19:54.030
You heard I was chief
operating officer at CMS,

00:19:54.030 --> 00:19:56.460
the largest payer in in the country.

00:19:56.460 --> 00:19:59.310
And so I really feel like operations

00:19:59.310 --> 00:20:02.040
and I want public health to make sure

00:20:02.040 --> 00:20:03.930
that they feel accountable and ownership

00:20:03.930 --> 00:20:06.930
over those operations
because we can't make folks

00:20:06.930 --> 00:20:08.580
and help folks protect themselves

00:20:08.580 --> 00:20:10.860
if we both don't get the operations right.

00:20:10.860 --> 00:20:12.663
And then the third is relationships.

00:20:13.560 --> 00:20:15.960
We're all humans at the end of the day.

00:20:15.960 --> 00:20:18.660
And it is remarkable about how sometimes

00:20:18.660 --> 00:20:20.970
we forget that and sitting down

00:20:20.970 --> 00:20:24.750
with folks and building
relationships with takes time.

00:20:24.750 --> 00:20:29.750
And I'll say the power of
showing up cannot be understated.

00:20:32.340 --> 00:20:34.290
We spend a lot of time showing up

00:20:34.290 --> 00:20:36.750
in communities that were
historically marginalized,

00:20:36.750 --> 00:20:39.750
showing up and listening

00:20:39.750 --> 00:20:41.790
was so important to trust building,

00:20:41.790 --> 00:20:43.080
to relationship building.

00:20:43.080 --> 00:20:44.670
I was lucky to have had three years

00:20:44.670 --> 00:20:46.800
of relationship building across the aisle

00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:48.420
before we went into the crisis.

00:20:48.420 --> 00:20:50.940
And that was really important

00:20:50.940 --> 00:20:53.374
so that we had a foundation,

00:20:53.374 --> 00:20:56.940
but we had to nurture that
foundation through the crisis.

00:20:56.940 --> 00:21:00.180
And it's why we had 99% of our seniors

00:21:00.180 --> 00:21:02.340
got vaccinated in North Carolina.

00:21:02.340 --> 00:21:03.780
North Carolina
- Extraordinary.

00:21:03.780 --> 00:21:06.698
- Voted to elect Donald Trump twice.

00:21:06.698 --> 00:21:09.810
Right, so it doesn't have to be partisan.

00:21:09.810 --> 00:21:11.550
No, I think that there,

00:21:11.550 --> 00:21:14.130
we tried to show a way where,

00:21:14.130 --> 00:21:16.451
where we could come together.

00:21:16.451 --> 00:21:18.990
We had all four of the two Democratic

00:21:18.990 --> 00:21:21.150
and two of our top Republican leaders,

00:21:21.150 --> 00:21:23.850
all four of them did
a vaccine video for us

00:21:23.850 --> 00:21:26.400
saying get vaccinated
right, in North Carolina.

00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:29.910
So we got to that place
because of relationships,

00:21:29.910 --> 00:21:31.530
but also because of transparency

00:21:31.530 --> 00:21:33.690
and good execution and
answering questions.

00:21:33.690 --> 00:21:35.520
But that relationship piece is critical.

00:21:35.520 --> 00:21:38.910
And that's, Roy Cooper
taught me that for sure.

00:21:38.910 --> 00:21:39.743
- How lucky you are,

00:21:39.743 --> 00:21:42.000
I will say having worked,
having spent 20 years

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:43.620
in Rhode Island where Gina Raimundo

00:21:43.620 --> 00:21:46.440
was our most recent governor,
she's extraordinary.

00:21:46.440 --> 00:21:48.780
It is amazing to watch these folks

00:21:48.780 --> 00:21:49.950
that know how to get things done.

00:21:49.950 --> 00:21:51.540
And I will say you are one of them

00:21:51.540 --> 00:21:54.270
who I have enjoyed
watching get things done.

00:21:54.270 --> 00:21:57.078
So you alluded in that
answer to two things

00:21:57.078 --> 00:21:59.350
that I know are, have both been long time

00:21:59.350 --> 00:22:00.570
focus of your career,

00:22:00.570 --> 00:22:02.940
but also are key to your work at CDC.

00:22:02.940 --> 00:22:05.580
One is around data and accuracy of data

00:22:05.580 --> 00:22:08.160
and accurate capture of demographics

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:10.950
so that we can address
equity and pay attention

00:22:10.950 --> 00:22:12.960
to the historically
marginalized communities

00:22:12.960 --> 00:22:15.990
that are experiencing
disproportionate burden of disease.

00:22:15.990 --> 00:22:17.370
The other is around community.

00:22:17.370 --> 00:22:19.498
And I think back to your
work at Doctors for America

00:22:19.498 --> 00:22:21.090
as an exemplar.

00:22:21.090 --> 00:22:23.370
I would love to hear your reflections

00:22:23.370 --> 00:22:25.680
on either or both of those.
- Sure.

00:22:25.680 --> 00:22:27.870
- Thinking about both kind
of that data equity issue,

00:22:27.870 --> 00:22:29.760
how you, I mean, really to me,

00:22:29.760 --> 00:22:31.290
what you did in North Carolina

00:22:31.290 --> 00:22:33.900
with getting demographic
data accurately measured

00:22:33.900 --> 00:22:35.593
was just transformative.

00:22:35.593 --> 00:22:37.733
How you're thinking about
that on a national level

00:22:37.733 --> 00:22:42.180
and then how you take those
lessons from DFA forwards?

00:22:42.180 --> 00:22:44.400
- Well, first I'm a data girl

00:22:44.400 --> 00:22:46.920
and so I think that data is the oxygen

00:22:46.920 --> 00:22:49.590
that powers our work,
particularly in public health.

00:22:49.590 --> 00:22:52.018
If you can't see problems,
you can't solve problems.

00:22:52.018 --> 00:22:54.840
We know that there are
disparities in our system,

00:22:54.840 --> 00:22:57.060
but until you get that accurate data,

00:22:57.060 --> 00:22:59.490
there's a lot of hand
waving of, ah, you know,

00:22:59.490 --> 00:23:01.497
that's not my patient,
that's not my right.

00:23:01.497 --> 00:23:04.774
So that clear, crisp data

00:23:04.774 --> 00:23:09.150
that changes action is really necessary.

00:23:09.150 --> 00:23:12.415
So let me talk about what
we did in North Carolina.

00:23:12.415 --> 00:23:14.880
Well, we said equity was at the center

00:23:14.880 --> 00:23:15.870
of what we were doing.

00:23:15.870 --> 00:23:19.588
And so we, it was just unacceptable to us

00:23:19.588 --> 00:23:22.530
to not be able to stratify
every piece of data

00:23:22.530 --> 00:23:25.350
that we had by race and ethnicity.

00:23:25.350 --> 00:23:27.060
We did it for gender and age too.

00:23:27.060 --> 00:23:29.820
And so you couldn't log a
vaccine in North Carolina

00:23:29.820 --> 00:23:32.550
without race and ethnicity.

00:23:32.550 --> 00:23:36.273
And we had 99% completion on
our race and ethnicity data.

00:23:37.140 --> 00:23:38.370
We won awards for that.

00:23:38.370 --> 00:23:39.300
Very proud of that work.

00:23:39.300 --> 00:23:41.437
And people fought us on it, said,

00:23:41.437 --> 00:23:43.380
"Oh, patients don't wanna answer that."

00:23:43.380 --> 00:23:45.274
or said, "It'll be too
slow for our systems."

00:23:45.274 --> 00:23:47.490
We managed through it, right?

00:23:47.490 --> 00:23:50.550
We came up with a bunch
of technology solutions,

00:23:50.550 --> 00:23:53.460
but patients want to answer
that question or frankly,

00:23:53.460 --> 00:23:54.720
why are they answering that question?

00:23:54.720 --> 00:23:57.510
It's in the EHR already, guys,
let's pull it out, right?

00:23:57.510 --> 00:24:00.570
So it was how do we think
about common sense solutions

00:24:00.570 --> 00:24:02.040
to make that possible?

00:24:02.040 --> 00:24:03.930
But then we have to use the data, right?

00:24:03.930 --> 00:24:05.950
I think a lot of times,
frankly, in public health,

00:24:05.950 --> 00:24:08.378
I love you all, but parsimony

00:24:08.378 --> 00:24:12.630
is not always our strong suit in,

00:24:12.630 --> 00:24:14.850
we just one more piece of
day, one more question,

00:24:14.850 --> 00:24:16.020
one more piece of data.

00:24:16.020 --> 00:24:18.390
We all have to collect race and ethnicity

00:24:18.390 --> 00:24:20.538
in the same way every
time for every program.

00:24:20.538 --> 00:24:22.830
So we have apples to apples comparison

00:24:22.830 --> 00:24:24.498
against across every program.

00:24:24.498 --> 00:24:26.392
It seems super basic,

00:24:26.392 --> 00:24:30.270
but we don't do it at the CDC right now.

00:24:30.270 --> 00:24:31.103
Guess who will?

00:24:31.103 --> 00:24:32.850
- You're also kind of not
allowed to do it, right?

00:24:32.850 --> 00:24:35.439
There's some data use
agreement issues right?

00:24:35.439 --> 00:24:38.010
- I don't think we can get past that.

00:24:38.010 --> 00:24:40.440
So I don't like to tolerate that, right?

00:24:40.440 --> 00:24:43.980
And so I'm always a how
do we find a way, right?

00:24:43.980 --> 00:24:48.980
But, so this is where I
also think we have to one,

00:24:49.303 --> 00:24:52.143
expect that, but then we have

00:24:52.143 --> 00:24:53.280
to use that data for action, right?

00:24:53.280 --> 00:24:56.070
And so it wasn't just
the collecting of it,

00:24:56.070 --> 00:24:57.983
then we're just admiring
the problem, right?

00:24:57.983 --> 00:25:00.039
It's the changing action.

00:25:00.039 --> 00:25:02.370
And what was powerful about it

00:25:02.370 --> 00:25:04.975
is that when we were
responding to the crisis

00:25:04.975 --> 00:25:06.480
on a day-to-day basis,

00:25:06.480 --> 00:25:08.527
we were looking at that
information and we said,

00:25:08.527 --> 00:25:10.867
"Why aren't vaccines over there?

00:25:10.867 --> 00:25:12.903
"Why is that community at a lower rate?

00:25:12.903 --> 00:25:16.267
"That hit that Latino community
over there at a lower rate?

00:25:16.267 --> 00:25:18.840
"Let's deploy resources over there."

00:25:18.840 --> 00:25:21.000
Or you pick up the phone
and say, did you know,

00:25:21.000 --> 00:25:22.157
did you know this is happening, right?

00:25:22.157 --> 00:25:24.120
And so you have to make that actionable

00:25:24.120 --> 00:25:27.120
and it's a self reinforcing. Yeah.

00:25:27.120 --> 00:25:28.590
So when you are actually using it

00:25:28.590 --> 00:25:30.750
and people are like,
oh, this is so useful,

00:25:30.750 --> 00:25:32.343
now I wanna collect the data.

00:25:32.343 --> 00:25:33.660
No questions asked.

00:25:33.660 --> 00:25:34.830
So it's great.

00:25:34.830 --> 00:25:37.250
Last thing I'll say on data
though that we're doing

00:25:37.250 --> 00:25:38.940
is in addition to standardization

00:25:38.940 --> 00:25:41.717
on how we collect, so it's most useful.

00:25:41.717 --> 00:25:45.180
We have to, all of us,
no matter where we sit,

00:25:45.180 --> 00:25:49.560
whether it's CDC or academia
or the health systems,

00:25:49.560 --> 00:25:52.080
we have to get away from the mindset

00:25:52.080 --> 00:25:54.633
of this data is mine.

00:25:56.040 --> 00:25:59.070
There's a lot of, well
I've done the hard work

00:25:59.070 --> 00:26:02.375
of collecting this data
and cleaning it out.

00:26:02.375 --> 00:26:03.575
- I get to publish from it, yeah.

00:26:03.575 --> 00:26:05.119
- I'm gonna publish my data

00:26:05.119 --> 00:26:06.810
and I appreciate that.

00:26:06.810 --> 00:26:09.535
But particularly I'm
working on with it CDC

00:26:09.535 --> 00:26:11.694
and with our state partners,

00:26:11.694 --> 00:26:16.200
we have to move away from
the data is mine mindset.

00:26:16.200 --> 00:26:19.620
Data is a national security asset, right?

00:26:19.620 --> 00:26:22.775
Is how we are going to protect this nation

00:26:22.775 --> 00:26:24.150
as we go forward.

00:26:24.150 --> 00:26:25.410
And we have to find ways

00:26:25.410 --> 00:26:29.095
to be able to share that
data across the board.

00:26:29.095 --> 00:26:31.260
We did it for covid.

00:26:31.260 --> 00:26:32.730
If we can do it for covid,

00:26:32.730 --> 00:26:34.500
we should do it across the board.

00:26:34.500 --> 00:26:36.810
We have to do it for
covid and flu and RSV.

00:26:36.810 --> 00:26:38.010
Let's start there.

00:26:38.010 --> 00:26:40.710
So right now we have great
data sharing and visibility.

00:26:40.710 --> 00:26:42.510
So if you wanna know
what's happening with covid

00:26:42.510 --> 00:26:43.953
in your backyard, you can know it,

00:26:43.953 --> 00:26:46.140
but you don't know about flu,

00:26:46.140 --> 00:26:47.310
you don't know about RSV

00:26:47.310 --> 00:26:48.900
in the same way.

00:26:48.900 --> 00:26:49.975
We're working to change that.

00:26:49.975 --> 00:26:51.893
And I'm working with our
state partners, right?

00:26:51.893 --> 00:26:53.040
Because to your point,

00:26:53.040 --> 00:26:55.440
there are different authorities and such.

00:26:55.440 --> 00:26:58.235
But we have to move
ourselves towards that path.

00:26:58.235 --> 00:27:00.975
And I'm looking for a
coalition of the willing

00:27:00.975 --> 00:27:03.074
to go on down that path with me

00:27:03.074 --> 00:27:05.400
and then I'll work with Congress

00:27:05.400 --> 00:27:09.512
to show why with that power
why that's so necessary.

00:27:09.512 --> 00:27:12.759
And that's needing help from
our health systems as well.

00:27:12.759 --> 00:27:16.530
So the data is mine mindset has to go,

00:27:16.530 --> 00:27:18.799
we need that data to
power healthy communities.

00:27:18.799 --> 00:27:22.560
And so we need to move
quickly in that direction.

00:27:22.560 --> 00:27:23.393
- Love that.

00:27:23.393 --> 00:27:25.050
So I'm gonna do one more question,

00:27:25.050 --> 00:27:27.270
then I'm gonna open it up. Dr. Juthani,

00:27:27.270 --> 00:27:29.430
I'm sure is both ready to be
a coalition of the willing,

00:27:29.430 --> 00:27:32.480
but also we're excited for
her to moderate questions.

00:27:32.480 --> 00:27:35.250
So my last question is a
slightly more personal one,

00:27:35.250 --> 00:27:36.840
knowing that this is a room of staff,

00:27:36.840 --> 00:27:38.430
faculty and students who came here

00:27:38.430 --> 00:27:42.300
to be inspired and to learn a
little bit about your journey.

00:27:42.300 --> 00:27:43.260
It's kind of a question about

00:27:43.260 --> 00:27:45.011
if you had the chance to go back in time

00:27:45.011 --> 00:27:47.700
and you have the opportunity now to speak

00:27:47.700 --> 00:27:50.580
to a bunch of aspiring leaders themselves.

00:27:50.580 --> 00:27:52.260
What do you wish you had learned?

00:27:52.260 --> 00:27:53.520
What are you thankful you learned?

00:27:53.520 --> 00:27:55.620
What do you encourage
the folks in the room

00:27:55.620 --> 00:27:59.130
to pursue as future public health leaders?

00:27:59.130 --> 00:28:00.534
- So two pieces of advice

00:28:00.534 --> 00:28:05.040
that I've been giving for a while now.

00:28:05.040 --> 00:28:07.380
Some one which I think I followed

00:28:07.380 --> 00:28:09.482
and one which I was disappointed I didn't.

00:28:09.482 --> 00:28:13.743
So I'll do the good first,
is be uncomfortable.

00:28:15.060 --> 00:28:17.559
I had this opportunity, my career has,

00:28:17.559 --> 00:28:20.190
so like now I can go back and tell

00:28:20.190 --> 00:28:21.615
like this nice story about

00:28:21.615 --> 00:28:22.895
- You're here.
- how it made sense.

00:28:22.895 --> 00:28:26.159
But my career like zigzagged a bit

00:28:26.159 --> 00:28:29.670
in terms of was this
the right step for me?

00:28:29.670 --> 00:28:31.920
And what I realize now in hindsight

00:28:31.920 --> 00:28:35.759
is that building differentiated
kinds of skill sets

00:28:35.759 --> 00:28:37.950
allowed me to open doors

00:28:37.950 --> 00:28:40.110
to different kinds of opportunities.

00:28:40.110 --> 00:28:42.840
So if I had just taken jobs

00:28:42.840 --> 00:28:45.597
that were fit for a doctor,

00:28:45.597 --> 00:28:48.240
I don't think I would have been able

00:28:48.240 --> 00:28:50.010
to make the progress in my career.

00:28:50.010 --> 00:28:53.220
There were times where I
was the communications lead,

00:28:53.220 --> 00:28:57.210
there was times where I was the data lead.

00:28:57.210 --> 00:28:58.952
There were times when

00:28:58.952 --> 00:29:01.860
it was my clinical
expertise that mattered.

00:29:01.860 --> 00:29:03.810
It was sometimes it was
my policy expertise.

00:29:03.810 --> 00:29:06.423
Sometimes I was just needing
to be a good manager.

00:29:07.290 --> 00:29:10.440
And so building different
kinds of skill sets,

00:29:10.440 --> 00:29:13.503
but that does require you to
step out of your comfort zone.

00:29:14.790 --> 00:29:17.070
Sometimes crisis pulls
you in that direction.

00:29:17.070 --> 00:29:20.100
And I would say sometimes
crisis is an opportunity.

00:29:20.100 --> 00:29:23.160
So think about moments of big change.

00:29:23.160 --> 00:29:25.530
For me, like I said, when
healthcare.gov didn't turn on,

00:29:25.530 --> 00:29:27.900
that was a big crisis
moment for the organization

00:29:27.900 --> 00:29:28.733
I was with.

00:29:28.733 --> 00:29:31.710
I got to step out
dramatically from where I was.

00:29:31.710 --> 00:29:33.270
I was doing physician payment models.

00:29:33.270 --> 00:29:35.250
Pretty typical what, you know,

00:29:35.250 --> 00:29:36.550
what folks would be thinking of

00:29:36.550 --> 00:29:41.550
where I went to work to
run the call center, right?

00:29:41.910 --> 00:29:44.100
So that was a hard conversation
to have with my mom

00:29:44.100 --> 00:29:47.294
when she was like, what are you
doing running a call center?

00:29:47.294 --> 00:29:49.290
But, and I was uncomfortable.

00:29:49.290 --> 00:29:50.970
I was like, I don't know
anything about a call center.

00:29:50.970 --> 00:29:51.870
But you know what,

00:29:52.815 --> 00:29:54.300
it was a fantastic learning experience

00:29:54.300 --> 00:29:56.040
to dive into something I didn't know.

00:29:56.040 --> 00:29:58.140
Think about leadership
and management, right?

00:29:58.140 --> 00:30:00.639
And that allowed me to start

00:30:00.639 --> 00:30:04.170
opening my mind a bit about skill building

00:30:04.170 --> 00:30:05.003
in different ways.

00:30:05.003 --> 00:30:05.836
So be uncomfortable.

00:30:06.900 --> 00:30:11.900
Second, the other part is
about asking great questions

00:30:12.270 --> 00:30:14.423
in whatever environment you're in.

00:30:14.423 --> 00:30:16.350
And I feel like this is where I fell down.

00:30:16.350 --> 00:30:17.490
Looking back now,

00:30:17.490 --> 00:30:18.690
I think there were moments

00:30:18.690 --> 00:30:22.140
where I didn't take
advantage of opportunities

00:30:22.140 --> 00:30:24.330
to ask questions of people I was around.

00:30:24.330 --> 00:30:25.410
'Cause I wanted everyone

00:30:25.410 --> 00:30:27.462
to think I already knew the answers.

00:30:27.462 --> 00:30:29.487
Everyone wants to.
- I can't imagine anyone

00:30:29.487 --> 00:30:31.200
in this room feels that way, right?

00:30:31.200 --> 00:30:33.993
- You wanna front and be like, I got this.

00:30:35.757 --> 00:30:38.340
And I missed opportunities

00:30:38.340 --> 00:30:42.720
to really understand
things earlier in my career

00:30:42.720 --> 00:30:45.480
when I should have understood 'em earlier

00:30:45.480 --> 00:30:48.510
if I just asked good
questions at the time.

00:30:48.510 --> 00:30:52.320
And I am so, I am always so enamored

00:30:52.320 --> 00:30:55.080
by young students who ask
really good questions.

00:30:55.080 --> 00:30:56.910
I'm like, I wish I could have asked

00:30:56.910 --> 00:30:58.650
those kind of questions
when I was your age.

00:30:58.650 --> 00:31:02.100
I would've been much smarter
sitting in the seat right now.

00:31:02.100 --> 00:31:04.080
Sometimes I had to do all the wrong things

00:31:04.080 --> 00:31:05.070
and learn the lesson.

00:31:05.070 --> 00:31:06.930
So ask the questions.

00:31:06.930 --> 00:31:09.270
Trust me if you have a question,
others have a question.

00:31:09.270 --> 00:31:11.160
But it's also the
opportunity to learn, right?

00:31:11.160 --> 00:31:13.830
No one expects you early in your career

00:31:13.830 --> 00:31:14.670
to know all the things.

00:31:14.670 --> 00:31:16.620
So please, like, ask good questions.

00:31:16.620 --> 00:31:19.047
Don't feel like you have
to know all the answers.

00:31:19.047 --> 00:31:20.543
And I would say that in any job.

00:31:20.543 --> 00:31:23.377
I would say the more I do in leadership,

00:31:23.377 --> 00:31:25.650
the way I lead is not,

00:31:25.650 --> 00:31:27.810
sometimes you need to
be a directive leader.

00:31:27.810 --> 00:31:29.670
But most of the time I'm going to the team

00:31:29.670 --> 00:31:31.710
to be like, what should we do?

00:31:31.710 --> 00:31:33.000
What should we do here?

00:31:33.000 --> 00:31:33.927
I will help you do it.

00:31:33.927 --> 00:31:36.884
And my job as the leader is to
break down barriers for you.

00:31:36.884 --> 00:31:39.863
If this is the right
thing to do, I'm gonna.

00:31:39.863 --> 00:31:42.870
If there's an authority problem,
I'm gonna fix it, right?

00:31:42.870 --> 00:31:45.270
If there's politics we need to
get through, how can I help?

00:31:45.270 --> 00:31:47.430
If we need to communicate,
what can I do, right?

00:31:47.430 --> 00:31:48.743
But I'm gonna go to the folks to say,

00:31:48.743 --> 00:31:51.267
what should I, what should I do?

00:31:51.267 --> 00:31:53.511
And it is to this day, the fact

00:31:53.511 --> 00:31:55.680
that I've been able to hone my ability

00:31:55.680 --> 00:31:57.420
to ask good questions,

00:31:57.420 --> 00:32:02.420
I think that has helped
me further my career.

00:32:02.490 --> 00:32:03.323
- I love that.

00:32:03.323 --> 00:32:04.320
And what a beautiful segue

00:32:04.320 --> 00:32:06.660
into a chance for questions to be asked.

00:32:06.660 --> 00:32:07.500
So thank you.

00:32:07.500 --> 00:32:08.580
Thank you for sharing those, Mandy.

00:32:08.580 --> 00:32:09.413
I think that's lovely.

00:32:09.413 --> 00:32:12.990
And I do really want
to kind of double click

00:32:12.990 --> 00:32:15.330
on the idea that careers
are not straight lines.

00:32:15.330 --> 00:32:17.550
And so for those who are
sitting out in the audience

00:32:17.550 --> 00:32:19.290
wondering what their next step is

00:32:19.290 --> 00:32:21.270
to not hesitate to take that risk

00:32:21.270 --> 00:32:22.103
and to not feel like

00:32:22.103 --> 00:32:23.760
you have to have it all figured out now.

00:32:23.760 --> 00:32:26.220
I feel like particularly for
kind of our newer generation,

00:32:26.220 --> 00:32:27.660
there's this sense that you need to know

00:32:27.660 --> 00:32:29.790
on the day when you start
your graduate program,

00:32:29.790 --> 00:32:31.050
what you're going to do next.

00:32:31.050 --> 00:32:32.070
And I don't, maybe you knew

00:32:32.070 --> 00:32:34.050
you were gonna do internal
medicine or public health,

00:32:34.050 --> 00:32:36.123
but I sure surprised myself.

00:32:37.140 --> 00:32:39.000
Thank you, Dr. Juthani.

00:32:39.000 --> 00:32:40.501
- Thank you both.

00:32:40.501 --> 00:32:41.670
We wanna have an opportunity for people

00:32:41.670 --> 00:32:43.260
to be able to ask questions.

00:32:43.260 --> 00:32:45.930
There are microphone rovers in the room,

00:32:45.930 --> 00:32:47.700
so if anybody has a question,

00:32:47.700 --> 00:32:49.890
feel free to raise your hand at this time.

00:32:49.890 --> 00:32:51.490
We have a few in the front here.

00:32:58.050 --> 00:32:59.460
- [Audience Member] Hi
Dean Ranney and Dr. Cohen,

00:32:59.460 --> 00:33:00.810
thank you so much for being here.

00:33:00.810 --> 00:33:02.430
I wanted to get your thoughts

00:33:02.430 --> 00:33:05.340
on online misinformation
and disinformation

00:33:05.340 --> 00:33:06.640
as a public health threat.

00:33:08.372 --> 00:33:10.950
- Little teeny topic.
- Yeah, very small.

00:33:10.950 --> 00:33:15.480
Yeah, so I know as early
as recent as last year,

00:33:15.480 --> 00:33:18.090
DHS tried to launch a
disinformation governance board

00:33:18.090 --> 00:33:20.007
and that ultimately failed.

00:33:20.007 --> 00:33:22.650
And we know a lot of disinformation

00:33:22.650 --> 00:33:24.993
is rooted in fear and misinformation.

00:33:27.324 --> 00:33:30.030
So how, what is the appropriate strategy

00:33:30.030 --> 00:33:31.200
to neutralize it, right?

00:33:31.200 --> 00:33:33.210
If there aren't any financial incentives

00:33:33.210 --> 00:33:35.460
or if the political
incentives aren't even there.

00:33:35.460 --> 00:33:38.193
So what can everyone do to combat that?

00:33:39.038 --> 00:33:42.900
- Yes, I think this is
really important topic.

00:33:42.900 --> 00:33:47.310
So a couple ways that I
thought about this, right?

00:33:47.310 --> 00:33:50.910
So I think those of us
with the good information

00:33:50.910 --> 00:33:53.340
need to drown out the
bad information, right?

00:33:53.340 --> 00:33:54.986
It's a flood the zone approach.

00:33:54.986 --> 00:33:59.610
So that so much good
information is out there

00:33:59.610 --> 00:34:03.930
that the misinformation is muted.

00:34:03.930 --> 00:34:06.090
But understand that in order for us

00:34:06.090 --> 00:34:07.770
to get good information out,

00:34:07.770 --> 00:34:09.450
we have to understand how folks

00:34:09.450 --> 00:34:11.062
are consuming that information

00:34:11.062 --> 00:34:14.670
and they're consuming it as entertainment.

00:34:14.670 --> 00:34:17.223
And we are not entertaining.

00:34:18.360 --> 00:34:19.596
I wish I was.

00:34:19.596 --> 00:34:22.290
- Right, but to us, you are.

00:34:22.290 --> 00:34:24.600
- So let's just be honest

00:34:24.600 --> 00:34:27.690
and I think we have to
meet folks where they are

00:34:27.690 --> 00:34:30.780
and think about different
ways to communicate

00:34:30.780 --> 00:34:31.860
the good information.

00:34:31.860 --> 00:34:34.080
So one, we have to just
do more of it, right?

00:34:34.080 --> 00:34:36.600
If everyone here sees some misinformation

00:34:36.600 --> 00:34:38.340
and then tweets the right information

00:34:38.340 --> 00:34:41.436
or sorry, X now.

00:34:41.436 --> 00:34:43.946
- Threads, get on threads, folks.

00:34:43.946 --> 00:34:45.540
Yeah, exactly.

00:34:45.540 --> 00:34:46.983
- So, right, right.

00:34:48.282 --> 00:34:49.802
And we need to, we can't,

00:34:49.802 --> 00:34:51.520
you are complicit if we
just like let that stand.

00:34:51.520 --> 00:34:53.670
So we need to flood the zone

00:34:53.670 --> 00:34:54.720
with the good information,

00:34:54.720 --> 00:34:55.980
but we also have to flood it

00:34:55.980 --> 00:34:59.130
with information that
feels engaging to folks.

00:34:59.130 --> 00:35:00.570
And I'm, to be honest,

00:35:00.570 --> 00:35:02.550
like I think we're all
still figuring this out

00:35:02.550 --> 00:35:04.162
and how to do this well.

00:35:04.162 --> 00:35:06.660
And like I think the
government particularly

00:35:06.660 --> 00:35:08.400
has a lot of challenges doing that

00:35:08.400 --> 00:35:10.200
for a lot of not good reasons,

00:35:10.200 --> 00:35:11.666
but some good reasons.

00:35:11.666 --> 00:35:16.639
And so we need to figure
out how to engage better.

00:35:16.639 --> 00:35:19.800
Far too often, and I was
saying this this morning

00:35:19.800 --> 00:35:22.586
to the team at the Fairhaven clinic,

00:35:22.586 --> 00:35:25.470
the team at CDC, I love them.

00:35:25.470 --> 00:35:28.560
They're so smart and
they're like, I'm so proud.

00:35:28.560 --> 00:35:31.563
I put some fantastic information
up on our website site.

00:35:34.380 --> 00:35:37.110
Like, oh boy, I haven't gone
to a website for information,

00:35:37.110 --> 00:35:38.250
I don't know when right?

00:35:38.250 --> 00:35:39.960
And I can't, I love public health

00:35:39.960 --> 00:35:40.800
and I go to the right.

00:35:40.800 --> 00:35:42.270
So that's not gonna be good.

00:35:42.270 --> 00:35:43.350
That's not good enough.

00:35:43.350 --> 00:35:46.290
We have to modernize
the way we communicate

00:35:46.290 --> 00:35:48.750
as a strategy related to disinformation.

00:35:48.750 --> 00:35:51.750
Then there's a whole
regulatory architecture

00:35:51.750 --> 00:35:52.980
that we need to think about.

00:35:52.980 --> 00:35:57.360
I am a hundred percent fully
supportive of free speech,

00:35:57.360 --> 00:36:01.110
but I think there's nothing
about free amplification

00:36:01.110 --> 00:36:02.490
that we need to think about.

00:36:02.490 --> 00:36:04.260
And so there's something
there in the middle

00:36:04.260 --> 00:36:07.770
where we can, everyone can
say the things they wanna say,

00:36:07.770 --> 00:36:09.840
but they don't all need
to be amplified in a way.

00:36:09.840 --> 00:36:12.727
And there's again, but
that's in the world of laws

00:36:12.727 --> 00:36:16.200
and regulations that folks
need to really think about

00:36:16.200 --> 00:36:18.300
that's broader than even just
public health information.

00:36:18.300 --> 00:36:19.351
It's bigger than that.

00:36:19.351 --> 00:36:22.837
So what we can do in
healthcare and public health

00:36:22.837 --> 00:36:25.080
is to flood the zone
with good information.

00:36:25.080 --> 00:36:28.560
We can be engaging, we can be trusted.

00:36:28.560 --> 00:36:31.140
And yes, people get their
information from social media,

00:36:31.140 --> 00:36:32.068
but the most trusted folks

00:36:32.068 --> 00:36:35.670
at survey after survey after survey,

00:36:35.670 --> 00:36:37.740
your doctor and your nurse, right?

00:36:37.740 --> 00:36:40.620
The nurse at your church and
your own personal doctor.

00:36:40.620 --> 00:36:43.062
So like every time you're
walking into a room with someone,

00:36:43.062 --> 00:36:45.870
you have an opportunity
to influence their lives.

00:36:45.870 --> 00:36:48.462
The number one reason
people don't get vaccinated,

00:36:48.462 --> 00:36:51.678
their doctor didn't
mention it at the visit,

00:36:51.678 --> 00:36:53.223
didn't say it was important.

00:36:54.630 --> 00:36:56.672
Not 'cause they thought it was,

00:36:56.672 --> 00:36:58.380
the doctor didn't mention it.

00:36:58.380 --> 00:37:00.730
They would've mentioned
it if it was important,

00:37:01.620 --> 00:37:02.970
but mention it, right?

00:37:02.970 --> 00:37:05.400
So every, we have to
take every opportunity.

00:37:05.400 --> 00:37:06.780
We don't have, I don't think we're ever

00:37:06.780 --> 00:37:08.580
gonna be quite as engaging online.

00:37:08.580 --> 00:37:10.830
So like let's take every
opportunity in person

00:37:10.830 --> 00:37:11.880
that we possibly can.

00:37:13.511 --> 00:37:16.113
- We have a question in
the front row over here.

00:37:18.480 --> 00:37:20.393
And then we'll go to the one in the back.

00:37:21.990 --> 00:37:23.280
- [Audience Member] Thank
you for the opportunity.

00:37:23.280 --> 00:37:26.185
And my question is for both of you.

00:37:26.185 --> 00:37:28.650
Part of the answer may be
that both of you are here.

00:37:28.650 --> 00:37:31.620
So we are aware that always assume

00:37:31.620 --> 00:37:33.990
that all of us know the importance

00:37:33.990 --> 00:37:37.020
of the relation between
institutes such as CDC

00:37:37.020 --> 00:37:41.520
with academic research
institutes like Yale.

00:37:41.520 --> 00:37:45.300
So if you can elaborate
more on this collaboration

00:37:45.300 --> 00:37:50.040
and relationship, like
to inform the decisions

00:37:50.040 --> 00:37:53.940
and the policies and also like
what would you do different

00:37:53.940 --> 00:37:56.520
to strengthen such
collaboration and relationship?

00:37:56.520 --> 00:37:58.170
Thank you.

00:37:58.170 --> 00:38:00.630
- Well obviously our academic partners

00:38:00.630 --> 00:38:05.160
are incredibly important
to train the future,

00:38:05.160 --> 00:38:09.570
the current and future
leaders of public health.

00:38:09.570 --> 00:38:11.550
So very important as we're thinking about

00:38:11.550 --> 00:38:14.790
what are those skillset
that folks need to build.

00:38:14.790 --> 00:38:17.532
But there's also really
important questions

00:38:17.532 --> 00:38:19.380
that get answered in academia.

00:38:19.380 --> 00:38:21.689
And we saw, frankly during the pandemic

00:38:21.689 --> 00:38:25.950
case number one of like
our academic partners

00:38:25.950 --> 00:38:30.950
being trusted voices to
share really good information

00:38:30.974 --> 00:38:33.540
and so, so critical.

00:38:33.540 --> 00:38:35.574
But it's also about
prioritization together

00:38:35.574 --> 00:38:37.530
and having a shared agenda

00:38:37.530 --> 00:38:38.760
on what are we trying to accomplish.

00:38:38.760 --> 00:38:40.920
I think there's more
work we need to do there

00:38:40.920 --> 00:38:42.540
between public health and academia.

00:38:42.540 --> 00:38:45.780
What are the things that CDC
needs to research and do?

00:38:45.780 --> 00:38:47.310
What are the things that academia,

00:38:47.310 --> 00:38:49.733
that CDC can't do that academia could do

00:38:49.733 --> 00:38:51.000
in different ways?

00:38:51.000 --> 00:38:54.540
I don't think we have as much
of a unified shared agenda

00:38:54.540 --> 00:38:55.410
as we need to.

00:38:55.410 --> 00:38:57.420
I think that's on the to-do list.

00:38:57.420 --> 00:38:58.470
I'm only a few, you know?

00:38:58.470 --> 00:39:00.150
- Yeah, we're both a couple of months in.

00:39:00.150 --> 00:39:02.079
Yeah, getting to, we're
getting to it, right?

00:39:02.079 --> 00:39:04.094
- But we need to do that

00:39:04.094 --> 00:39:04.927
because I think there
are certain things CDC

00:39:04.927 --> 00:39:06.279
needs to stop doing.

00:39:06.279 --> 00:39:07.860
I think there are certain things

00:39:07.860 --> 00:39:09.853
that academia needs to pick
up to do, but what, right?

00:39:09.853 --> 00:39:13.470
And we have to have that collaboration

00:39:13.470 --> 00:39:14.310
back and forth to be like,

00:39:14.310 --> 00:39:17.220
okay, if you've got this,
then we're gonna nod

00:39:17.220 --> 00:39:18.053
and we'll go over there.

00:39:18.053 --> 00:39:20.100
'Cause there's so many important
things we all need to do.

00:39:20.100 --> 00:39:21.840
There's too much work in the world, right?

00:39:21.840 --> 00:39:24.393
So I think there's more
work we need to do.

00:39:25.829 --> 00:39:26.963
But Dean, getting over to you.

00:39:27.973 --> 00:39:28.973
- Exactly what you just said.

00:39:28.973 --> 00:39:29.806
I think that we think of ourselves at,

00:39:29.806 --> 00:39:31.500
we can think of ourselves as extenders

00:39:31.500 --> 00:39:32.610
of each other's skillsets.

00:39:32.610 --> 00:39:34.489
We do sit in slightly different places

00:39:34.489 --> 00:39:37.117
within the ecosystem of
public health and healthcare.

00:39:37.117 --> 00:39:40.080
And so how do we work in partnership

00:39:40.080 --> 00:39:42.000
to do the things that maybe CDC

00:39:42.000 --> 00:39:44.010
or our state and local health departments

00:39:44.010 --> 00:39:45.810
wish they could do, but can't.

00:39:45.810 --> 00:39:46.860
They don't have the staff,

00:39:46.860 --> 00:39:48.480
they don't have the infrastructure.

00:39:48.480 --> 00:39:51.480
Maybe they don't have the
very specialized expertise.

00:39:51.480 --> 00:39:54.213
And then vice versa, our
partnership back, CDC

00:39:54.213 --> 00:39:56.760
and state and local health departments

00:39:56.760 --> 00:40:01.680
have an unbelievable platform
to disseminate, to implement,

00:40:01.680 --> 00:40:04.140
to actually change policies and procedures

00:40:04.140 --> 00:40:07.110
on the ground and have
an incredible workforce

00:40:07.110 --> 00:40:09.450
that is present in almost every community

00:40:09.450 --> 00:40:10.770
across the United States.

00:40:10.770 --> 00:40:11.850
And then if we look globally,

00:40:11.850 --> 00:40:13.757
certainly there are
partnerships there as well.

00:40:13.757 --> 00:40:16.590
And so knowing kind of
how then on our side,

00:40:16.590 --> 00:40:17.733
the information gets shared

00:40:17.733 --> 00:40:20.610
and made actionable is huge.

00:40:20.610 --> 00:40:21.690
The the last part that I'll say

00:40:21.690 --> 00:40:24.660
that I see is an existing
source of partnership

00:40:24.660 --> 00:40:27.630
that we can grow is around grants,

00:40:27.630 --> 00:40:29.760
around support of not just research,

00:40:29.760 --> 00:40:31.590
but also implementation.

00:40:31.590 --> 00:40:35.173
For those who aren't aware,
CDC is a tremendous funder

00:40:35.173 --> 00:40:36.870
of not just research,

00:40:36.870 --> 00:40:40.230
but also on the ground
implementation work.

00:40:40.230 --> 00:40:42.210
And I think that it
behooves us as academics

00:40:42.210 --> 00:40:44.460
to think about how we
can be better partners

00:40:44.460 --> 00:40:46.890
in that space as well.

00:40:46.890 --> 00:40:48.210
But the shared agenda is something

00:40:48.210 --> 00:40:50.580
that I'm excited to
work on going forwards.

00:40:50.580 --> 00:40:52.800
And I think we're gonna have
a bunch of opportunities

00:40:52.800 --> 00:40:53.910
to think a little differently

00:40:53.910 --> 00:40:56.217
as we move into this new
ecosystem of public health

00:40:56.217 --> 00:40:58.800
and healthcare about how we can amplify

00:40:58.800 --> 00:41:00.250
and extend each other's work.

00:41:02.130 --> 00:41:03.840
- I have a question in
the back, I believe.

00:41:03.840 --> 00:41:04.800
Yeah, go ahead.

00:41:04.800 --> 00:41:05.640
- [Nora] Sure.

00:41:05.640 --> 00:41:07.950
Dr. Cohen, so nice to have you here.

00:41:07.950 --> 00:41:09.210
Thank you for coming.

00:41:09.210 --> 00:41:10.260
My name is Nora Alsabi.

00:41:10.260 --> 00:41:13.326
I'm a first year at the Yale
School of Public Health.

00:41:13.326 --> 00:41:15.373
So my question actually has two parts.

00:41:15.373 --> 00:41:18.030
First, I'm just wondering,

00:41:18.030 --> 00:41:19.950
what are some of the strategies

00:41:19.950 --> 00:41:23.760
adapted from covid that
are kind of being used

00:41:23.760 --> 00:41:25.290
to be implemented in the future

00:41:25.290 --> 00:41:29.117
or the preparedness phase
for like any future pandemic?

00:41:29.117 --> 00:41:33.150
Second part is actually one
that I'm very interested in.

00:41:33.150 --> 00:41:35.683
I'm just wondering if there
has been any initiatives

00:41:35.683 --> 00:41:39.810
or your review on preparing programming

00:41:39.810 --> 00:41:43.140
or any other kind of part of initiatives

00:41:43.140 --> 00:41:47.717
to address immigrant health
issues in the United States,

00:41:47.717 --> 00:41:52.050
and what would that look
like in the upcoming years?

00:41:52.050 --> 00:41:53.580
- Great, so on the first,

00:41:53.580 --> 00:41:55.200
in terms of lessons learned from covid,

00:41:55.200 --> 00:41:57.590
there are so many, so many, so many.

00:41:57.590 --> 00:42:00.090
But I'll go back to some core ones,

00:42:00.090 --> 00:42:02.166
which is we need data infrastructure

00:42:02.166 --> 00:42:04.608
to see issues and solve problems.

00:42:04.608 --> 00:42:09.608
I think we, my first effort
as North Carolina secretary

00:42:09.780 --> 00:42:11.969
was an effort we called Kill the Fax.

00:42:11.969 --> 00:42:13.530
And that's because we were getting

00:42:13.530 --> 00:42:16.620
lab data by fax, ugh, ugh,

00:42:16.620 --> 00:42:19.860
when we went into a historic pandemic.

00:42:19.860 --> 00:42:23.837
So I had 250 people entering
information into a computer

00:42:23.837 --> 00:42:25.380
until we killed the fax,

00:42:25.380 --> 00:42:28.650
but right, so we've built
systems now, fantastic.

00:42:28.650 --> 00:42:31.050
But we built them for covid.

00:42:31.050 --> 00:42:33.030
But there's some railroad
tracks there, right?

00:42:33.030 --> 00:42:34.530
So we've laid some tracks

00:42:34.530 --> 00:42:37.920
on how to build pipes
and think about this.

00:42:37.920 --> 00:42:39.540
And so CDC is being funded

00:42:39.540 --> 00:42:41.190
for a huge data monetization effort

00:42:41.190 --> 00:42:43.770
that's across CDC, it's to the states,

00:42:43.770 --> 00:42:45.150
a lot of work being done.

00:42:45.150 --> 00:42:48.000
We have to get systems
that talk to each other

00:42:48.000 --> 00:42:49.890
across healthcare and public health.

00:42:49.890 --> 00:42:53.250
So a lot, I mean, absolutely
fundamentally have to do that.

00:42:53.250 --> 00:42:56.670
If we want to call this
a public health system,

00:42:56.670 --> 00:43:00.090
data integration is like
absolutely fundamental.

00:43:00.090 --> 00:43:01.950
Then there is all of the work related

00:43:01.950 --> 00:43:05.173
to how we recognize public
health as a team sport.

00:43:05.173 --> 00:43:07.890
We saw how we needed to
bring in different folks

00:43:07.890 --> 00:43:10.290
and clarifying roles and responsibilities

00:43:10.290 --> 00:43:13.290
in peace time versus crisis time.

00:43:13.290 --> 00:43:15.210
What are the right roles
and responsibilities

00:43:15.210 --> 00:43:17.010
for the health delivery system?

00:43:17.010 --> 00:43:19.860
I'll say in North Carolina,
our major hospital systems

00:43:19.860 --> 00:43:21.330
were hugely important.

00:43:21.330 --> 00:43:24.420
And I again, give huge credit to them,

00:43:24.420 --> 00:43:27.360
I would convene these competitors, right?

00:43:27.360 --> 00:43:30.060
They're business competitors
across our state.

00:43:30.060 --> 00:43:32.010
I'd convene them every Monday morning

00:43:32.010 --> 00:43:34.133
to make sure that we were
aligned and executing.

00:43:34.133 --> 00:43:37.197
But that's not the norm, right?

00:43:37.197 --> 00:43:38.550
That's in crisis.

00:43:38.550 --> 00:43:40.557
So how do we think about
roles and responsibility

00:43:40.557 --> 00:43:42.397
not in crisis as well?

00:43:42.397 --> 00:43:45.810
And then the communication
and trust building.

00:43:45.810 --> 00:43:48.240
You can't build trust in a crisis.

00:43:48.240 --> 00:43:50.220
You have to be there throughout.

00:43:50.220 --> 00:43:52.500
So the trust building has to start now

00:43:52.500 --> 00:43:55.350
for a crisis we may have
10 years from now, right?

00:43:55.350 --> 00:43:57.390
In the communities that we need to be in.

00:43:57.390 --> 00:43:59.790
So I heard an interesting
point this morning at Fairhaven

00:43:59.790 --> 00:44:02.812
that, you know, we're
spending a lot of effort

00:44:02.812 --> 00:44:04.650
on the mental health crisis and they said,

00:44:04.650 --> 00:44:06.930
you know what, more than
the mental health crisis,

00:44:06.930 --> 00:44:09.690
we're actually seeing dental health

00:44:09.690 --> 00:44:12.870
as one of the most significant
needs for the community here.

00:44:12.870 --> 00:44:16.332
And I was like, oh, well
one, oh, that's so hard

00:44:16.332 --> 00:44:18.780
and, but so important.

00:44:18.780 --> 00:44:21.968
So let's start, let's
listen as leaders, right?

00:44:21.968 --> 00:44:23.640
That's how you build trust.

00:44:23.640 --> 00:44:26.880
You listen to what are the
community's first top priorities.

00:44:26.880 --> 00:44:27.990
Let's solve that.

00:44:27.990 --> 00:44:29.100
So that in five years,

00:44:29.100 --> 00:44:32.148
if we have another crisis,
hopefully not right?

00:44:32.148 --> 00:44:34.410
That we are there,

00:44:34.410 --> 00:44:36.384
we're solving problems that
matter to the community.

00:44:36.384 --> 00:44:38.847
So like we have to, like now is when

00:44:38.847 --> 00:44:41.160
we have to do the preparation

00:44:41.160 --> 00:44:44.820
and in terms of our immigrant communities,

00:44:44.820 --> 00:44:46.740
what I love about public health

00:44:46.740 --> 00:44:48.627
and I'm so proud of CDC

00:44:48.627 --> 00:44:50.250
and only being there three months,

00:44:50.250 --> 00:44:51.570
I take no credit for this,

00:44:51.570 --> 00:44:54.978
but it is honestly the most
equity focused organization

00:44:54.978 --> 00:44:57.060
that I have been part of.

00:44:57.060 --> 00:44:58.800
And I'm very proud of
the North Carolina work.

00:44:58.800 --> 00:45:01.020
But it was a lot of like new work.

00:45:01.020 --> 00:45:02.850
When I come to CDC and I see it,

00:45:02.850 --> 00:45:05.280
the equity work is embedded
everywhere in a great way.

00:45:05.280 --> 00:45:06.690
And they think about populations

00:45:06.690 --> 00:45:10.440
and they are thinking about migrant

00:45:10.440 --> 00:45:12.088
and their health at all times

00:45:12.088 --> 00:45:14.400
and thinking about how do we make sure

00:45:14.400 --> 00:45:15.960
we're understanding what they need

00:45:15.960 --> 00:45:16.800
and think about it.

00:45:16.800 --> 00:45:18.780
So for example, I was just on the phone

00:45:18.780 --> 00:45:21.480
with folks in New York who are seeing

00:45:21.480 --> 00:45:25.410
a lot of new immigrants
and migrants that are there

00:45:25.410 --> 00:45:26.940
and how can we help them

00:45:26.940 --> 00:45:28.200
from an infectious disease place,

00:45:28.200 --> 00:45:29.850
but also from housing
and things like that.

00:45:29.850 --> 00:45:31.680
So CDC, like that's,

00:45:31.680 --> 00:45:34.092
I give them a ton of credit
for doing incredible work.

00:45:34.092 --> 00:45:37.821
The resources are what is missing there.

00:45:37.821 --> 00:45:40.741
It's not the passion,
it's not the strategy,

00:45:40.741 --> 00:45:43.950
it's resources in order to make sure

00:45:43.950 --> 00:45:45.630
that we can help all those communities.

00:45:45.630 --> 00:45:47.790
- And if I can just jump
in real, two things.

00:45:47.790 --> 00:45:48.990
One is around the migrants.

00:45:48.990 --> 00:45:50.640
It is just to put a finer point on that,

00:45:50.640 --> 00:45:52.652
partnerships, I recently
had a lovely conversation

00:45:52.652 --> 00:45:56.580
with the head of health and
human services in Massachusetts.

00:45:56.580 --> 00:45:58.470
They're also struggling
with the same problem.

00:45:58.470 --> 00:45:59.700
And it really is the partnerships

00:45:59.700 --> 00:46:03.400
that it's not all on
DHHS helping to provide

00:46:04.380 --> 00:46:07.020
a livelihood and a life to allow folks

00:46:07.020 --> 00:46:08.160
to start to integrate.

00:46:08.160 --> 00:46:10.050
So often, we think about health

00:46:10.050 --> 00:46:11.340
as sitting off in this bubble

00:46:11.340 --> 00:46:14.400
versus being part of this
much larger ecosystem

00:46:14.400 --> 00:46:17.610
of issues and things that
help to create health.

00:46:17.610 --> 00:46:18.900
The other thing is you were talking about

00:46:18.900 --> 00:46:20.610
the data modernization initiative.

00:46:20.610 --> 00:46:22.140
Going back to your question,

00:46:22.140 --> 00:46:23.466
something that we can do in academia

00:46:23.466 --> 00:46:27.492
is to help tell the
story of why CDC matters

00:46:27.492 --> 00:46:30.150
and of why their initiatives matter.

00:46:30.150 --> 00:46:32.580
So I bring up the data
modernization initiative

00:46:32.580 --> 00:46:34.412
anytime I talk to a policymaker.

00:46:34.412 --> 00:46:39.090
CDC has asked for 165
million, more or less,

00:46:39.090 --> 00:46:39.923
more than that.

00:46:39.923 --> 00:46:41.430
You're going up, you're going up

00:46:41.430 --> 00:46:42.780
from Michelle's estimate, okay?

00:46:42.780 --> 00:46:43.740
That's less than it costs

00:46:43.740 --> 00:46:45.532
to put Epic into one
hospital system. Yeah.

00:46:45.532 --> 00:46:47.190
- That's why my number's bigger.

00:46:47.190 --> 00:46:48.810
- Good.

00:46:48.810 --> 00:46:50.430
But just to kind of put that in context,

00:46:50.430 --> 00:46:51.570
people don't think about it.

00:46:51.570 --> 00:46:52.686
If you're talking to policymakers,

00:46:52.686 --> 00:46:54.068
they don't think about data

00:46:54.068 --> 00:46:56.700
as court of public health
the way that we do.

00:46:56.700 --> 00:46:59.130
And so we have that
chance to tell the story.

00:46:59.130 --> 00:47:00.870
We also have the chance to tell the story

00:47:00.870 --> 00:47:02.430
of why CDC matters,

00:47:02.430 --> 00:47:04.732
why this work matters in
those community relationships

00:47:04.732 --> 00:47:06.870
and to help create the connections

00:47:06.870 --> 00:47:09.630
that then allow you to
hear that dental health

00:47:09.630 --> 00:47:11.670
is the primary concern of the community

00:47:11.670 --> 00:47:15.270
that's being treated at this
major FQHC in New Haven.

00:47:15.270 --> 00:47:18.330
So just as an addition
to the beautiful question

00:47:18.330 --> 00:47:20.530
that you asked earlier,
and Nora, thank you.

00:47:21.690 --> 00:47:24.780
- Dr. Cohen, we are killing
the fax in Connecticut.

00:47:24.780 --> 00:47:27.252
We haven't quite killed it,
but your data modernization

00:47:27.252 --> 00:47:29.700
efforts are helping us get there.

00:47:29.700 --> 00:47:31.830
Okay, we have, I know several
other questions I saw.

00:47:31.830 --> 00:47:33.330
Okay, right here on the front.

00:47:34.230 --> 00:47:35.280
- [Audience Member]
Great, thank you so much.

00:47:35.280 --> 00:47:36.810
Thank you for being here.

00:47:36.810 --> 00:47:39.600
I had a question on
behalf of a person I know

00:47:39.600 --> 00:47:42.720
who's on dialysis and they were wondering

00:47:42.720 --> 00:47:45.301
why it's not required
for healthcare workers

00:47:45.301 --> 00:47:49.980
to wear N95 respirators
in healthcare settings.

00:47:49.980 --> 00:47:52.290
Especially when decades of research

00:47:52.290 --> 00:47:55.050
showed that respirators are more effective

00:47:55.050 --> 00:47:58.410
than surgical mass at
preventing airborne diseases.

00:47:58.410 --> 00:48:03.410
And with like 60% of covid infections,

00:48:03.810 --> 00:48:07.260
asymptomatic and hospital
acquired infections

00:48:07.260 --> 00:48:09.868
four times higher,

00:48:09.868 --> 00:48:12.393
have a four times higher
risk of mortality.

00:48:13.968 --> 00:48:15.090
- So great question.

00:48:15.090 --> 00:48:16.612
So we have different teams who

00:48:16.612 --> 00:48:20.490
are looking at some of
these issues right now,

00:48:20.490 --> 00:48:22.620
and I think updating some
of the guidance, right?

00:48:22.620 --> 00:48:24.452
So I think you wanna look at,

00:48:24.452 --> 00:48:26.760
we look at a couple of factors
when thinking about it.

00:48:26.760 --> 00:48:28.906
Sure, is an N95 gonna certainly filter out

00:48:28.906 --> 00:48:30.467
more things than a surgical mask?

00:48:30.467 --> 00:48:32.700
Yes, we know how those work, right?

00:48:32.700 --> 00:48:34.140
If you have something that's fit tested

00:48:34.140 --> 00:48:36.508
and the different material.

00:48:36.508 --> 00:48:38.970
So, but the question
is what are the things

00:48:38.970 --> 00:48:41.100
that you need to wear an N95 for?

00:48:41.100 --> 00:48:41.933
And what are the things

00:48:41.933 --> 00:48:44.790
where surgical barrier masks make sense?

00:48:44.790 --> 00:48:46.428
And you wanna look both at the way

00:48:46.428 --> 00:48:51.428
the infection is spread, but
also what is the risk, right?

00:48:51.990 --> 00:48:56.370
So we spread a lot of
things that are aerosolized,

00:48:56.370 --> 00:48:58.620
but there there are
different levels of risk.

00:48:59.670 --> 00:49:04.050
It is good news in 2023 where
we are related to covid.

00:49:04.050 --> 00:49:08.700
97% of us have either had it
or have been vaccinated by it.

00:49:08.700 --> 00:49:11.910
So we aren't in the same
place we were in 2020

00:49:11.910 --> 00:49:13.830
when we had an entire population

00:49:13.830 --> 00:49:15.930
that's never been exposed.

00:49:15.930 --> 00:49:18.810
Now I wouldn't, I have to be
on brand for CDC director.

00:49:18.810 --> 00:49:20.288
I'm glad we got to this.

00:49:20.288 --> 00:49:24.810
Our immunity is going down,
the virus is changing.

00:49:24.810 --> 00:49:26.982
Please all get your updated covid vaccine.

00:49:26.982 --> 00:49:29.163
Even those of us who are,

00:49:31.259 --> 00:49:32.910
I almost was saying I'm
as young as you, I'm not,

00:49:32.910 --> 00:49:34.050
I'm getting my vaccine.

00:49:34.050 --> 00:49:36.120
But please, everyone at every age

00:49:36.120 --> 00:49:37.980
should get it as well as your flu shot.

00:49:37.980 --> 00:49:40.260
But my point is, is that we have to think

00:49:40.260 --> 00:49:42.270
about levels of risk for all of those

00:49:42.270 --> 00:49:44.070
different kinds of diseases versus

00:49:44.070 --> 00:49:46.890
in terms of what is the
protection level that we need.

00:49:46.890 --> 00:49:49.710
And so, those are the kinds of research

00:49:49.710 --> 00:49:50.580
that the team goes through.

00:49:50.580 --> 00:49:52.620
We actually have an
independent advisory committee

00:49:52.620 --> 00:49:55.230
that is going through some
of that work right now

00:49:55.230 --> 00:49:57.750
to understand the data
and what, how do you map

00:49:57.750 --> 00:50:00.570
to the right protection because it's both

00:50:00.570 --> 00:50:02.280
how is it transmitted,

00:50:02.280 --> 00:50:05.220
but also like what is the
risk to all of the patients?

00:50:05.220 --> 00:50:08.250
And we sort of need to
balance all of those things.

00:50:08.250 --> 00:50:09.300
- Great, thank you.

00:50:09.300 --> 00:50:10.570
I know there are a few others.

00:50:10.570 --> 00:50:13.113
Right behind the last person who asked.

00:50:15.720 --> 00:50:17.730
- [Audience Member] Hi,
thank you for being here.

00:50:17.730 --> 00:50:20.160
This question is addressed to both of you,

00:50:20.160 --> 00:50:22.906
but it's based on something
that Dr. Cohen said.

00:50:22.906 --> 00:50:27.666
You said earlier that data is
a national securities asset

00:50:27.666 --> 00:50:31.470
and we need to move away
from a data is mine mindset.

00:50:31.470 --> 00:50:33.383
And so I was wondering whether
you think there's a place

00:50:33.383 --> 00:50:36.690
for the CDC or just
general US health policy

00:50:36.690 --> 00:50:39.510
to maybe require this data sharing,

00:50:39.510 --> 00:50:41.430
especially in the realm of public health

00:50:41.430 --> 00:50:44.970
because it's so important
to just our general health

00:50:44.970 --> 00:50:47.820
in general for the whole country?

00:50:47.820 --> 00:50:49.662
- The short answer is yes.

00:50:49.662 --> 00:50:52.263
So I am, and I talk about
it with members of congress

00:50:52.263 --> 00:50:55.419
whenever I have a conversation with them.

00:50:55.419 --> 00:51:00.064
Now I understand why we
have to have a balance here.

00:51:00.064 --> 00:51:03.060
Just like balance in the last
question I was talking about,

00:51:03.060 --> 00:51:05.580
we wanna collect data
to have that visibility.

00:51:05.580 --> 00:51:07.260
We need to protect privacy.

00:51:07.260 --> 00:51:09.346
Absolutely, I think that is important too.

00:51:09.346 --> 00:51:12.240
A lot of the data and
that can be actionable,

00:51:12.240 --> 00:51:15.263
can be de-identified in
the way that we collect it

00:51:15.263 --> 00:51:17.850
and use it and it still be actionable.

00:51:17.850 --> 00:51:20.048
There are some places where
that shouldn't be true

00:51:20.048 --> 00:51:22.106
in terms of case identification,

00:51:22.106 --> 00:51:24.510
but those are the things
we're trying to work through

00:51:24.510 --> 00:51:26.400
with Congress and help them understand,

00:51:26.400 --> 00:51:29.370
de-identify data is what
we're really talking about

00:51:29.370 --> 00:51:31.866
as the asset that we need to understand

00:51:31.866 --> 00:51:33.720
and respond to threats.

00:51:33.720 --> 00:51:36.346
And I think we're making
some progress there.

00:51:36.346 --> 00:51:39.060
But you know, unfortunately

00:51:39.060 --> 00:51:42.546
these modernized data systems
and all, they're expensive.

00:51:42.546 --> 00:51:46.113
Also, just candidly, data is a,

00:51:47.755 --> 00:51:52.320
gives you market power
in the healthcare system.

00:51:52.320 --> 00:51:55.830
And so, there are different
forces that pull upon this.

00:51:55.830 --> 00:51:57.240
So we're gonna try to tell the story

00:51:57.240 --> 00:51:59.730
of why we need this data.

00:51:59.730 --> 00:52:01.530
I am spent most of my career

00:52:01.530 --> 00:52:03.030
on the health delivery side.

00:52:03.030 --> 00:52:04.200
We have to do this in a way

00:52:04.200 --> 00:52:07.080
that is not burdensome to
the health delivery system.

00:52:07.080 --> 00:52:10.290
We need to pull and extract
the data out of EHRs.

00:52:10.290 --> 00:52:12.763
We cannot ask for additional
manual work for folks.

00:52:12.763 --> 00:52:16.429
So this is where my,
I'm wearing my CMS hat

00:52:16.429 --> 00:52:18.660
for a second, right?

00:52:18.660 --> 00:52:21.000
CMS has a lot of mechanisms

00:52:21.000 --> 00:52:22.380
in which we collect data already.

00:52:22.380 --> 00:52:25.980
Public health needs to
fit into that mechanism.

00:52:25.980 --> 00:52:27.720
So we need to make sure
it's not burdensome.

00:52:27.720 --> 00:52:32.040
We need to make sure that
the privacy is protected.

00:52:32.040 --> 00:52:34.830
But yes, additional authorities
are absolutely needed

00:52:34.830 --> 00:52:36.150
to keep the country safe.

00:52:36.150 --> 00:52:39.330
- And that gets to your
simplicity precept around trust,

00:52:39.330 --> 00:52:41.910
which is that we have
to make it really easy

00:52:41.910 --> 00:52:45.900
for people to gather, share, and use data.

00:52:45.900 --> 00:52:47.730
I will say that there is actually

00:52:47.730 --> 00:52:51.150
any of you who have CDC,
NIH or NSF funded grants

00:52:51.150 --> 00:52:54.300
know that we are already
expected to share data,

00:52:54.300 --> 00:52:56.280
to deposit whatever data we can

00:52:56.280 --> 00:52:57.714
in a shared data repository,

00:52:57.714 --> 00:53:00.274
whether it's quantitative or qualitative.

00:53:00.274 --> 00:53:02.775
Once you have removed
those markers of privacy,

00:53:02.775 --> 00:53:05.190
I also think, to your point around that,

00:53:05.190 --> 00:53:07.260
that privacy and ethical question,

00:53:07.260 --> 00:53:08.550
it's why it's so important for us

00:53:08.550 --> 00:53:12.270
to think about this being done
not on an individual basis,

00:53:12.270 --> 00:53:14.250
but on an individual locality basis,

00:53:14.250 --> 00:53:17.370
but across states,
regions, and the country.

00:53:17.370 --> 00:53:18.540
Because what we often find

00:53:18.540 --> 00:53:20.370
is that some of our smallest groups,

00:53:20.370 --> 00:53:22.950
their data gets suppressed over and over.

00:53:22.950 --> 00:53:25.800
So it limits our ability
to look with granularity

00:53:25.800 --> 00:53:27.960
to Mandy's earlier point about data equity

00:53:27.960 --> 00:53:31.470
at, for example, native Hawaiian

00:53:31.470 --> 00:53:33.540
and Pacific Islander populations.

00:53:33.540 --> 00:53:34.920
Because in a given locale,

00:53:34.920 --> 00:53:36.300
the numbers may be so small

00:53:36.300 --> 00:53:37.800
that then it becomes identifiable.

00:53:37.800 --> 00:53:40.830
So it's a way to think about
kind of improving equity

00:53:40.830 --> 00:53:42.428
as well as data sharing.

00:53:42.428 --> 00:53:45.960
But this goes back to your
work in North Carolina.

00:53:45.960 --> 00:53:48.300
You were so committed to creating spaces

00:53:48.300 --> 00:53:50.610
where that even if
perhaps the full dataset

00:53:50.610 --> 00:53:52.378
couldn't be shared, that the summaries

00:53:52.378 --> 00:53:54.136
of the data could be,

00:53:54.136 --> 00:53:57.060
which allows access in in other ways.

00:53:57.060 --> 00:53:59.190
And then I think helped
you to be a convener

00:53:59.190 --> 00:54:00.656
of data sources

00:54:00.656 --> 00:54:02.758
- Yes, and I was gonna say, I love,

00:54:02.758 --> 00:54:05.517
we have amazing scientists at the CDC

00:54:05.517 --> 00:54:07.260
and they are incredible.

00:54:07.260 --> 00:54:09.990
They want precision and sometimes

00:54:09.990 --> 00:54:11.670
that's not gonna be possible

00:54:11.670 --> 00:54:13.890
in the messy world that we live in.

00:54:13.890 --> 00:54:17.539
And so thinking about, well,
if we can't collect everything,

00:54:17.539 --> 00:54:19.110
what's half a loaf?

00:54:19.110 --> 00:54:20.970
I'll take that to start.

00:54:20.970 --> 00:54:23.520
So we're trying to get to that place.

00:54:23.520 --> 00:54:25.260
- Okay. We've got about six minutes left,

00:54:25.260 --> 00:54:26.700
so I'm gonna try to take two,

00:54:26.700 --> 00:54:28.130
but let's take one question.

00:54:28.130 --> 00:54:29.760
There was one in the back there

00:54:29.760 --> 00:54:31.350
and then we'll try to come
back here to the front

00:54:31.350 --> 00:54:32.790
if we can fit it in.

00:54:32.790 --> 00:54:33.930
In the back.

00:54:33.930 --> 00:54:35.450
- [Lena] Hello. Oh, hello.

00:54:35.450 --> 00:54:38.070
Thank you for being here, Dr. Cohen.

00:54:38.070 --> 00:54:40.170
I can only imagine your busy schedule

00:54:40.170 --> 00:54:42.060
and taking the time to be here for us

00:54:42.060 --> 00:54:46.800
is very gratitude
instilling, but thank you.

00:54:46.800 --> 00:54:49.860
My name is Lena Jones, I'm a
third year in Yale undergrad.

00:54:49.860 --> 00:54:53.700
And I wanted to ask about how,

00:54:53.700 --> 00:54:58.666
as a woman of data you
plan for data's ethical use

00:54:58.666 --> 00:55:01.440
because I know that
there's this long history

00:55:01.440 --> 00:55:05.970
of data being collected and
intended for wonderful purposes,

00:55:05.970 --> 00:55:08.746
and then it's actual application

00:55:08.746 --> 00:55:10.710
ends up harming the communities

00:55:10.710 --> 00:55:13.140
that it was most intended to serve.

00:55:13.140 --> 00:55:14.970
- Yeah, great question.

00:55:14.970 --> 00:55:15.900
This is part of the,

00:55:15.900 --> 00:55:18.106
you need to protect
privacy as we go, right?

00:55:18.106 --> 00:55:20.026
So a lot of times we can do this,

00:55:20.026 --> 00:55:21.850
do the data collection

00:55:21.850 --> 00:55:25.710
without making sure that we're
identifying any individual.

00:55:25.710 --> 00:55:27.900
That's the first.

00:55:27.900 --> 00:55:31.830
But I think this goes to deep
seated decades upon decades

00:55:31.830 --> 00:55:35.250
where the structural way
in which we both collect

00:55:35.250 --> 00:55:37.402
and use data there, as we know,

00:55:37.402 --> 00:55:39.889
there is racism built into our structures.

00:55:39.889 --> 00:55:44.050
And so then when we take,
even the way we collect data,

00:55:44.050 --> 00:55:47.160
having disparities built into it,

00:55:47.160 --> 00:55:50.040
and then we try to use
these new tools like AI

00:55:50.040 --> 00:55:51.960
or our large language models,

00:55:51.960 --> 00:55:54.900
like we perpetuate those disparities.

00:55:54.900 --> 00:55:57.330
So we have to really be
thinking about it proactively

00:55:57.330 --> 00:55:58.500
on the front end.

00:55:58.500 --> 00:56:00.660
But honestly, when I hear your question,

00:56:00.660 --> 00:56:03.390
it always goes back to trust, right?

00:56:03.390 --> 00:56:05.586
Are we having the
conversation on the front end

00:56:05.586 --> 00:56:07.500
and helping the community see

00:56:07.500 --> 00:56:09.090
is like when we ask these questions

00:56:09.090 --> 00:56:09.990
and we're collecting this data,

00:56:09.990 --> 00:56:12.450
it's to help come back to you

00:56:12.450 --> 00:56:13.560
to make sure that it's better

00:56:13.560 --> 00:56:16.523
and actually deliver on that.

00:56:16.523 --> 00:56:18.648
It's why knowing that dental

00:56:18.648 --> 00:56:23.280
is the most important issue
here is really powerful.

00:56:23.280 --> 00:56:25.900
So we could be off collecting
data on covid vaccines,

00:56:25.900 --> 00:56:28.680
but everyone's like, well, really?

00:56:28.680 --> 00:56:29.880
Why aren't you collecting data

00:56:29.880 --> 00:56:32.430
on what's going on
related to my oral health?

00:56:32.430 --> 00:56:36.270
And so I think it's also having
those trusting conversations

00:56:36.270 --> 00:56:38.100
with folks and then also making sure

00:56:38.100 --> 00:56:39.480
that you're always being value add

00:56:39.480 --> 00:56:40.787
and we have to check
ourselves in public health.

00:56:40.787 --> 00:56:44.760
'Cause I think we come in with
a lot of idealistic thoughts

00:56:44.760 --> 00:56:47.100
about what a community needs

00:56:47.100 --> 00:56:48.870
and that we really need to make sure

00:56:48.870 --> 00:56:50.580
we're taking a beat to listen.

00:56:50.580 --> 00:56:52.110
- And here I'll just
say if this is an issue

00:56:52.110 --> 00:56:54.090
that interests you, check out Datahaven,

00:56:54.090 --> 00:56:55.770
which is a local organization

00:56:55.770 --> 00:56:58.500
run by one of our YSPH
graduates, Mark Abraham,

00:56:58.500 --> 00:57:01.688
that does a lovely job
collecting, sharing and empowering

00:57:01.688 --> 00:57:05.250
communities to use local
data around health.

00:57:05.250 --> 00:57:06.300
So a lovely example.

00:57:06.300 --> 00:57:07.650
- Great.
- Yeah, it's super cool.

00:57:07.650 --> 00:57:09.360
It's super cool.

00:57:09.360 --> 00:57:11.330
- Okay, we have time for
one quick last question

00:57:11.330 --> 00:57:12.750
right here in the front, please.

00:57:12.750 --> 00:57:13.583
Last question.

00:57:13.583 --> 00:57:15.240
- [Rachel] Hi, okay, I will make it quick.

00:57:15.240 --> 00:57:16.073
Hi, I'm Rachel.

00:57:16.073 --> 00:57:18.210
I'm a second year at the
Yale School of Public Health.

00:57:18.210 --> 00:57:20.100
I actually work for the
commissioner over the summer,

00:57:20.100 --> 00:57:21.810
so it's great to see you all in person.

00:57:21.810 --> 00:57:26.610
My question is, what do you
see as the role of the CDC

00:57:26.610 --> 00:57:30.130
in advancing whole Person
health on a national scale?

00:57:30.130 --> 00:57:31.710
And how will that dovetail

00:57:31.710 --> 00:57:34.590
the localized state
efforts already in motion?

00:57:34.590 --> 00:57:38.424
- Big question to end and I'll
go back to where I started.

00:57:38.424 --> 00:57:41.129
I actually think public
health is one piece of that,

00:57:41.129 --> 00:57:43.546
and it has to be a team sport.

00:57:43.546 --> 00:57:45.930
I think there's a lot of
pieces in public health,

00:57:45.930 --> 00:57:47.460
but I'll go back to like public health

00:57:47.460 --> 00:57:52.110
is not resourced to fulfill
its current mission,

00:57:52.110 --> 00:57:54.840
which is identifying and
responding to threats.

00:57:54.840 --> 00:57:57.900
And so it, we do, but there is a lot

00:57:57.900 --> 00:58:00.826
of public health
principles that I wanna map

00:58:00.826 --> 00:58:03.840
to all of the levers across healthcare.

00:58:03.840 --> 00:58:05.490
And that's not just
health delivery system.

00:58:05.490 --> 00:58:07.242
As I said, social services
has to be part of it.

00:58:07.242 --> 00:58:09.510
Education has to be part of it.

00:58:09.510 --> 00:58:11.330
Transportation has to be part of it.

00:58:11.330 --> 00:58:14.220
So this is a US government approach

00:58:14.220 --> 00:58:17.070
and frankly, gotta get the
private sector, academia, right?

00:58:17.070 --> 00:58:19.020
It's a team sport here.

00:58:19.020 --> 00:58:22.230
And that's, for me, like
that's the exciting part

00:58:22.230 --> 00:58:24.390
is how do we knit these pieces together?

00:58:24.390 --> 00:58:25.830
What I'm trying to do at CDC

00:58:25.830 --> 00:58:28.260
is make us the best collaborator

00:58:28.260 --> 00:58:30.450
we absolutely possibly could be.

00:58:30.450 --> 00:58:33.300
Now, I think data is
the power behind that.

00:58:33.300 --> 00:58:35.220
Because like that visualization,

00:58:35.220 --> 00:58:38.550
but it's building a muscle of
collaboration is different.

00:58:38.550 --> 00:58:41.280
How like, candidly, like CDC

00:58:41.280 --> 00:58:45.660
does not have right now
someone who owns engagement,

00:58:45.660 --> 00:58:48.000
stakeholder engagement or they,

00:58:48.000 --> 00:58:49.530
we wanna call it
something else, but right,

00:58:49.530 --> 00:58:51.420
but doesn't have that function.

00:58:51.420 --> 00:58:53.386
But if we're gonna be
the best collaborator,

00:58:53.386 --> 00:58:55.380
and if this is a team sport,

00:58:55.380 --> 00:58:57.330
we absolutely have to build
that muscle differently.

00:58:57.330 --> 00:58:59.566
So those are the kinds of
things I'm working on at CDC,

00:58:59.566 --> 00:59:02.670
but we have a lot to offer
in and of itself, right?

00:59:02.670 --> 00:59:05.610
With the data evidence and best practices

00:59:05.610 --> 00:59:07.470
so that we can go to someone like CMS

00:59:07.470 --> 00:59:09.540
and be like, you know what
you should be paying for

00:59:09.540 --> 00:59:11.386
is X, not Y, right?

00:59:11.386 --> 00:59:14.010
Or we can go to SAMHSA
and say, you know what?

00:59:14.010 --> 00:59:16.610
We, we've studied this best
practice in suicide prevention.

00:59:16.610 --> 00:59:18.660
This is where you should put your money.

00:59:18.660 --> 00:59:19.493
And they do.

00:59:19.493 --> 00:59:20.326
And we're doing that right now

00:59:20.326 --> 00:59:22.106
so that we're trying to model out

00:59:22.106 --> 00:59:25.710
those best opportunities
for collaboration,

00:59:25.710 --> 00:59:27.210
make sure public health is strong

00:59:27.210 --> 00:59:29.520
in what it can offer in
terms of data evidence

00:59:29.520 --> 00:59:31.470
best practices, and then that can,

00:59:31.470 --> 00:59:32.820
but don't leave it there.

00:59:32.820 --> 00:59:35.626
That's to get to the folks
who then deliver the services

00:59:35.626 --> 00:59:37.342
so that they know how to use

00:59:37.342 --> 00:59:39.210
the data evidence best practices.

00:59:39.210 --> 00:59:41.460
And so like making those connection points

00:59:41.460 --> 00:59:43.626
is like very important to me.

00:59:43.626 --> 00:59:47.010
And so we're gonna be doing
a lot of that at the CDC,

00:59:47.010 --> 00:59:48.630
but we can't do it alone.

00:59:48.630 --> 00:59:50.490
And it'll be a team sport

00:59:50.490 --> 00:59:52.740
and everyone's on team health.

00:59:52.740 --> 00:59:53.760
So thank you.

00:59:53.760 --> 00:59:55.620
- Well, I cannot think of anyone better

00:59:55.620 --> 00:59:58.650
to exemplify leadership in
public health than Dr. Cohen.

00:59:58.650 --> 01:00:00.840
I cannot thank you enough
on behalf of all of us.

01:00:00.840 --> 01:00:03.603
We do have a small, so
a thank you to Mandy.

01:00:11.580 --> 01:00:12.500
Thank you.

01:00:12.500 --> 01:00:14.280
So I wanna thank Claire,

01:00:14.280 --> 01:00:15.600
who, if those don't know her,

01:00:15.600 --> 01:00:19.470
is the brains and heart
who works with Howie

01:00:19.470 --> 01:00:22.200
and allows him to have
brains and heart as well.

01:00:22.200 --> 01:00:23.450
Not that he didn't have them before.

01:00:23.450 --> 01:00:25.170
but thank you Claire for your help.

01:00:25.170 --> 01:00:27.106
And we have a small gift bag for you.

01:00:27.106 --> 01:00:29.670
As well as for Dr. Juthani,

01:00:29.670 --> 01:00:31.226
as a thank you to both of you

01:00:31.226 --> 01:00:34.110
also exemplifying
leadership and public health

01:00:34.110 --> 01:00:35.180
on behalf of our state.

01:00:35.180 --> 01:00:37.106
Again, just a very warm thank you

01:00:37.106 --> 01:00:40.713
to both of them for their
presence, leadership, and grace.

01:00:49.500 --> 01:00:50.520
- Thank you all for coming.

01:00:50.520 --> 01:00:53.373
And for this inspirational
conversation today.

